**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Promise.all() takes an iterable of promises and returns a single Promise that fulfills when ALL input promises fulfill, or rejects if ANY reject (fail-fast). Returns results in input order, not completion order. Non-promise values are included as-is.

Syntax: Promise.all(iterable)

Best for: Independent async tasks that must all succeed.

Example:
```javascript
async function getPrice() {
  const [choice, prices] = await Promise.all([
    promptForDishChoice(),
    fetchPrices(),
  ]);
  return prices[choice];
}

const p1 = Promise.resolve(3);
const p2 = 42;
const p3 = new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 100, 'foo'));
Promise.all([p1, p2, p3]).then((values) => console.log(values)); // [3, 42, 'foo']
```

Fail-fast behavior: If any promise rejects, Promise.all rejects immediately with that reason, even if others would succeed later.

