Promise.race() takes an iterable and returns a Promise that settles with the state of the FIRST promise to settle (whether fulfilled or rejected). Empty iterable = promise stays pending forever.

Syntax: Promise.race(iterable)

Use cases: Request timeouts, first-to-complete patterns, racing multiple data sources.

Example — Request Timeout Pattern:
```javascript
const data = Promise.race([
  fetch('/api'),
  new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('Request timed out')), 5000);
  }),
])
  .then((res) => res.json())
  .catch((err) => displayError(err));
```

Example — Basic race:
```javascript
const promise1 = new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 500, 'one'));
const promise2 = new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 100, 'two'));
Promise.race([promise1, promise2]).then((value) => console.log(value)); // 'two'
```

Difference from Promise.any(): race() takes first settled (including rejections); any() takes first fulfilled only.

