SharedArrayBuffer enables true shared memory between the main thread and workers. Both agents read and write the same underlying bytes.

Security requirements (must have both HTTP headers):
- Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
- Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp

Constructor:
```javascript
const sab = new SharedArrayBuffer(1024); // 1024 bytes
const growableSab = new SharedArrayBuffer(1024, { maxByteLength: 8192 }); // growable
```

Sharing with worker:
```javascript
// Main thread
const sab = new SharedArrayBuffer(4);
const view = new Int32Array(sab);
view[0] = 42;
worker.postMessage(sab); // both sides share same memory

// Worker
self.onmessage = (event) => {
  const sharedBuffer = event.data;
  const view = new Int32Array(sharedBuffer);
  console.log(view[0]); // 42 — sees main thread's value
  view[0] = 100;        // modifies shared memory
};
```

Check availability:
```javascript
if (crossOriginIsolated) {
  const buffer = new SharedArrayBuffer(16);
  myWorker.postMessage(buffer);
} else {
  const buffer = new ArrayBuffer(16); // fallback
}
```

Grow shared buffer:
```javascript
const sab = new SharedArrayBuffer(1024, { maxByteLength: 8192 });
sab.grow(2048);
sab.byteLength; // 2048
```

WARNING: Raw reads/writes to SharedArrayBuffer without Atomics can cause race conditions. Always use Atomics for concurrent access.

