JavaScript is single-threaded. The execution model uses an event loop to handle async operations without blocking.

Core components:
1. Call Stack (LIFO) — tracks active function calls
2. Microtask Queue — Promise callbacks, queueMicrotask() — HIGH priority
3. Task Queue — setTimeout, setInterval, I/O — LOWER priority
4. Heap — object memory allocation

Event loop order per iteration:
1. Execute one task from task queue
2. Drain entire microtask queue (including newly added microtasks)
3. Render/paint if needed
4. Repeat

Example — execution order:
```javascript
console.log('Script start');
setTimeout(() => console.log('setTimeout 1'), 0);
Promise.resolve()
  .then(() => {
    console.log('Promise 1');
    return Promise.resolve();
  })
  .then(() => console.log('Promise 2'));
queueMicrotask(() => console.log('queueMicrotask'));
console.log('Script end');

// Output:
// Script start
// Script end
// Promise 1
// queueMicrotask
// Promise 2
// setTimeout 1
```

Run-to-completion guarantee: each task runs fully before the next starts — no preemption.

Agent clusters and shared memory: windows and dedicated workers can share a SharedArrayBuffer. Cross-origin windows cannot.

