queueMicrotask() schedules a function to run as a microtask — after current task, before browser regains control of event loop.

Syntax: queueMicrotask(callback)
Return: undefined

Microtask vs setTimeout:
```
queueMicrotask() → microtask queue (before next task)
setTimeout()     → task queue (after event loop yields)
```

Practical use — consistent async behavior (cached vs fresh data):
```javascript
MyElement.prototype.loadData = function(url) {
  if (this._cache[url]) {
    queueMicrotask(() => { // use microtask so behavior matches async fetch
      this._setData(this._cache[url]);
      this.dispatchEvent(new Event('load'));
    });
  } else {
    fetch(url)
      .then((res) => res.arrayBuffer())
      .then((data) => {
        this._cache[url] = data;
        this._setData(data);
        this.dispatchEvent(new Event('load'));
      });
  }
};
```

Use when:
- Need code to run async but before next render
- Making cached path behave identical to async path
- DOM batching before paint
- Library/framework cleanup tasks

Do NOT use for: true delays, post-render work (use setTimeout instead).

