One of the most common performance mistakes in async JavaScript: awaiting independent promises sequentially instead of concurrently.

BAD — Sequential (slow):
```javascript
async function slowVersion() {
  const user = await fetchUser();     // waits 1s
  const posts = await fetchPosts();   // waits another 1s (doesn't need user!)
  const friends = await fetchFriends(); // waits another 1s
  // Total: ~3 seconds
  return { user, posts, friends };
}
```

GOOD — Parallel (fast):
```javascript
async function fastVersion() {
  const [user, posts, friends] = await Promise.all([
    fetchUser(),
    fetchPosts(),
    fetchFriends(),
  ]);
  // Total: ~1 second (all run at once)
  return { user, posts, friends };
}
```

GOOD — Parallel with dependency:
```javascript
async function withDependency() {
  const user = await fetchUser(); // must come first
  const [posts, friends] = await Promise.all([
    fetchPosts(user.id), // depend on user
    fetchFriends(user.id), // depend on user
  ]);
  return { user, posts, friends };
}
```

Rule: If operation B does not need the result of A to start, run A and B with Promise.all(). Only use sequential await when there is a true data dependency.

