Callback hell is deeply nested callback functions that create a 'pyramid of doom' — hard to read, maintain, and error-handle.

BAD — callback hell:
```javascript
doSomething(function(result) {
  doSomethingElse(result, function(newResult) {
    doThirdThing(newResult, function(finalResult) {
      console.log('Got the final result: ' + finalResult);
    }, failureCallback);
  }, failureCallback);
}, failureCallback);
```

GOOD — promise chain:
```javascript
doSomething()
  .then((result) => doSomethingElse(result))
  .then((newResult) => doThirdThing(newResult))
  .then((finalResult) => console.log('Got the final result: ' + finalResult))
  .catch(failureCallback);
```

BEST — async/await:
```javascript
async function foo() {
  try {
    const result = await doSomething();
    const newResult = await doSomethingElse(result);
    const finalResult = await doThirdThing(newResult);
    console.log('Got the final result: ' + finalResult);
  } catch (error) {
    failureCallback(error);
  }
}
```

Fix: Replace nested callbacks with promise chains or async/await. Each callback in pyramid becomes one .then().

