A classic JavaScript race condition: multiple callbacks or timers modifying a shared variable in unpredictable order.

PROBLEM:
```javascript
let counter = 0;

function increment() {
  const current = counter;       // read
  setTimeout(() => {
    counter = current + 1;       // write happens later, based on stale read
  }, Math.random() * 100);
}

for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  increment();
}

setTimeout(() => {
  console.log(counter); // could be 1-10, not guaranteed to be 10
}, 500);
```

WHY: Each setTimeout callback captures `current` at time of call. By the time it runs, other callbacks may have already written, and this one overwrites with stale value.

FIX — use synchronous increment:
```javascript
let counter = 0;

function increment() {
  counter += 1; // synchronous — no defer
}

for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  increment();
}
console.log(counter); // always 10
```

FIX — if async is needed, serialize with Promise chain:
```javascript
let counterPromise = Promise.resolve(0);

function increment() {
  counterPromise = counterPromise.then((current) => current + 1);
}

for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) increment();
counterPromise.then((v) => console.log(v)); // always 10
```

