**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Closures in loops that capture a mutable variable (var-declared) instead of the loop iteration value cause all callbacks to see the final value of the variable.

PROBLEM — var in for loop:
```javascript
const funcs = [];
for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  funcs.push(function() {
    console.log(i); // captures var i by reference
  });
}
funcs.forEach(f => f()); // prints 5 5 5 5 5 (not 0 1 2 3 4)
```

PROBLEM — setTimeout in loop:
```javascript
for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  setTimeout(function() {
    console.log(i); // all print 5
  }, i * 100);
}
```

FIX 1 — use let (block-scoped, creates new binding per iteration):
```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  setTimeout(function() {
    console.log(i); // prints 0 1 2 3 4
  }, i * 100);
}
```

FIX 2 — IIFE to capture value:
```javascript
for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  (function(j) {
    setTimeout(function() {
      console.log(j); // prints 0 1 2 3 4
    }, j * 100);
  })(i);
}
```

FIX 3 — use forEach (each callback has its own parameter scope):
```javascript
[0, 1, 2, 3, 4].forEach((i) => {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), i * 100);
});
```

