Reading and writing a SharedArrayBuffer between workers without using Atomics creates data races — the read-modify-write sequence is not atomic and can be interleaved between threads.

PROBLEM — non-atomic increment:
```javascript
// Worker 1
const view = new Int32Array(sharedBuffer);
const old = view[0];     // read
view[0] = old + 1;       // write — NOT atomic; worker 2 may write between read and write

// Worker 2 (simultaneously)
const old = view[0];     // may read same value as Worker 1
view[0] = old + 1;       // both increment from same old value — one increment lost
```

FIX — use Atomics.add() for atomic increment:
```javascript
// Worker 1
Atomics.add(view, 0, 1); // read-modify-write is atomic — uninterruptible

// Worker 2
Atomics.add(view, 0, 1); // safe to call concurrently
```

FIX — use compareExchange for conditional update:
```javascript
function atomicIncrement(view, index) {
  let oldVal;
  do {
    oldVal = Atomics.load(view, index);
  } while (Atomics.compareExchange(view, index, oldVal, oldVal + 1) !== oldVal);
  // retry until CAS succeeds (no other writer changed value between load and CAS)
}
```

FIX — use wait/notify for coordination:
```javascript
// Producer
Atomics.store(view, 0, 42);
Atomics.notify(view, 0, 1); // wake consumer

// Consumer
Atomics.wait(view, 0, 0); // block until value != 0
console.log(Atomics.load(view, 0)); // 42
```

