A singleton initialized lazily in an async context can be initialized multiple times if multiple callers check before initialization completes.

PROBLEM:
```javascript
let instance = null;

async function getInstance() {
  if (!instance) {                    // multiple callers pass this check
    instance = await createInstance(); // all create new instances
  }
  return instance; // returns DIFFERENT instances to different callers
}

// Two concurrent calls — both see instance === null
const [a, b] = await Promise.all([getInstance(), getInstance()]);
console.log(a === b); // false — two different instances created
```

FIX — store the Promise itself as the singleton:
```javascript
let instancePromise = null;

async function getInstance() {
  if (!instancePromise) {
    instancePromise = createInstance(); // store Promise, not result
    // second caller sees non-null instancePromise immediately
  }
  return instancePromise; // both callers await same Promise
}

const [a, b] = await Promise.all([getInstance(), getInstance()]);
console.log(a === b); // true — same instance
```

FIX — module-level singleton with top-level await:
```javascript
// singleton.js
const instance = await createInstance(); // module evaluated once
export default instance;

// consumer.js
import instance from './singleton.js'; // always same instance
```

