Node.js EventEmitter enables loosely-coupled async event handling, allowing multiple concurrent handlers without shared state.

```javascript
const { EventEmitter } = require('events');

class DataPipeline extends EventEmitter {
  async process(data) {
    try {
      const result = await this.transform(data);
      this.emit('data', result);
    } catch (err) {
      this.emit('error', err);
    }
  }

  async transform(data) {
    // async transformation
    return data.map((x) => x * 2);
  }
}

const pipeline = new DataPipeline();
pipeline.on('data', (result) => console.log('Processed:', result));
pipeline.on('error', (err) => console.error('Failed:', err));

// Process multiple datasets concurrently
await Promise.all([
  pipeline.process([1, 2, 3]),
  pipeline.process([4, 5, 6]),
]);
```

Once listener — handles event exactly once:
```javascript
pipeline.once('ready', () => {
  console.log('Pipeline ready — this runs only once');
});
```

Max listeners warning: Node.js warns if more than 10 listeners are added to the same event (memory leak signal).
```javascript
pipeline.setMaxListeners(20); // increase limit if needed
```

Pattern for async event handlers:
```javascript
pipeline.on('data', async (result) => {
  try {
    await saveToDatabase(result);
  } catch (err) {
    // EventEmitter does not await async handlers
    // errors here are unhandled — must catch explicitly
    console.error(err);
  }
});
```

