**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Producer-consumer pattern decouples data production from consumption, allowing different rates without blocking either side.

```javascript
class AsyncQueue {
  constructor() {
    this._queue = [];
    this._waiting = [];
  }

  enqueue(item) {
    if (this._waiting.length > 0) {
      const resolve = this._waiting.shift();
      resolve(item);
    } else {
      this._queue.push(item);
    }
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this._queue.length > 0) {
      return Promise.resolve(this._queue.shift());
    }
    return new Promise((resolve) => this._waiting.push(resolve));
  }
}

// Producer
async function producer(queue) {
  for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    await delay(100);
    queue.enqueue(i);
    console.log('Produced:', i);
  }
}

// Consumer
async function consumer(queue) {
  for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    const item = await queue.dequeue(); // waits if empty
    await delay(200); // slower consumer
    console.log('Consumed:', item);
  }
}

const queue = new AsyncQueue();
await Promise.all([producer(queue), consumer(queue)]);
```

Real-world use: rate limiting API calls, batching database writes, streaming data processing.

