Unhandled promise rejections crash Node.js (since v15) and log warnings in browsers. This is a common source of silent failures in concurrent code.

PROBLEM:
```javascript
async function riskyOperation() {
  throw new Error('Something went wrong');
}

// Fire-and-forget with no error handling
riskyOperation(); // rejection is unhandled — crashes Node.js v15+

// In Promise.all — one rejection swallows the rest
Promise.all([
  fetchData(),
  riskyOperation(), // rejects
  fetchMore(),      // rejection from above propagates, fetchMore may be ignored
]); // entire Promise.all rejects with no .catch()
```

FIX — always attach .catch():
```javascript
riskyOperation().catch((err) => {
  console.error('Background task failed:', err);
});

// Or use void with catch for intentional fire-and-forget
void riskyOperation().catch(console.error);
```

FIX — global handler as safety net (not a substitute):
```javascript
// Browser
window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', (event) => {
  console.error('Unhandled rejection:', event.reason);
  event.preventDefault();
});

// Node.js
process.on('unhandledRejection', (reason, promise) => {
  console.error('Unhandled Rejection at:', promise, 'reason:', reason);
});
```

FIX — use Promise.allSettled when partial failure is OK:
```javascript
// Instead of Promise.all (fail-fast)
const results = await Promise.allSettled([
  fetchData(),
  riskyOperation(),
  fetchMore(),
]);
// All three run regardless of rejections
```

