BroadcastChannel enables publish-subscribe messaging between browsing contexts (windows, tabs, iframes, workers) of the same origin. One sender, many receivers.

API:
- `new BroadcastChannel(name)` — subscribe to named channel
- `channel.postMessage(data)` — broadcast to all listeners except self
- `channel.close()` — unsubscribe and allow garbage collection
- `channel.onmessage` / `channel.addEventListener('message', handler)` — receive messages
- `channel.onmessageerror` — fired when message cannot be deserialized

Example:
```javascript
// Tab 1 — Sender
const channel = new BroadcastChannel('app_state');
channel.postMessage({ type: 'USER_LOGGED_OUT', userId: '123' });

// Tab 2 — Receiver (same origin)
const channel = new BroadcastChannel('app_state');
channel.addEventListener('message', (event) => {
  if (event.data.type === 'USER_LOGGED_OUT') {
    clearUserSession();
    redirectToLogin();
  }
});

// Always clean up
channel.close();
```

Use cases: cross-tab auth logout sync, shared cart state, cache invalidation across tabs, real-time notifications. Sender does NOT receive its own messages. Restricted to same origin — different from server-sent events or WebSocket.

