MessageChannel creates a two-ended message pipe — port1 and port2 are linked MessagePort objects. Data sent through one arrives at the other. Used for direct bidirectional communication between contexts.

Pattern:
```javascript
// Main thread
const channel = new MessageChannel();
const iframe = document.querySelector('iframe');

iframe.addEventListener('load', () => {
  // Keep port1, transfer port2 to iframe
  channel.port1.onmessage = (e) => console.log('Reply:', e.data);
  iframe.contentWindow.postMessage('init', '*', [channel.port2]);
});

// Send message to iframe via port
channel.port1.postMessage({ type: 'DATA', payload: myData });

// Inside iframe
window.addEventListener('message', (e) => {
  const port = e.ports[0]; // received port2
  port.onmessage = (msg) => {
    // handle message from main thread
    port.postMessage({ status: 'received', data: msg.data });
  };
});
```

With Web Workers:
```javascript
const { port1, port2 } = new MessageChannel();
worker.postMessage('setup', [port2]); // transfer port2 to worker

port1.onmessage = (e) => console.log('Worker says:', e.data);
port1.postMessage('hello worker');
```

Key: port2 is a transferable — after transfer, main thread loses access. Provides decoupled, direct channel without relying on postMessage string matching.

