Promise.withResolvers() returns { promise, resolve, reject } — resolve and reject are exposed outside the executor. Useful when promise needs to be resolved by events that happen later, especially in loops.

Syntax:
```javascript
const { promise, resolve, reject } = Promise.withResolvers();
```

Equivalent to (but cleaner than):
```javascript
let resolve, reject;
const promise = new Promise((res, rej) => { resolve = res; reject = rej; });
```

Use case — transforming Node.js stream to async iterable:
```javascript
async function* readableToAsyncIterable(stream) {
  let { promise, resolve, reject } = Promise.withResolvers();

  stream.on('error', reject);
  stream.on('end', resolve);
  stream.on('readable', resolve);

  while (stream.readable) {
    await promise;
    let chunk;
    while ((chunk = stream.read())) yield chunk;
    // Re-create for next wait cycle
    ({ promise, resolve, reject } = Promise.withResolvers());
  }
}
```

Use case — manual async gate:
```javascript
const { promise: ready, resolve: markReady } = Promise.withResolvers();

// Elsewhere, when system is ready
markReady();

// Multiple waiters can all await the same promise
await ready;
```

Advantage over new Promise(): resolve/reject usable across event listeners without closure variable hoisting.

**Scenario (Finance):** Implementing this pattern to handle transactions efficiently.

