AbortController creates a controller + AbortSignal pair. Pass the signal to async operations; call controller.abort() to cancel all of them.

Basic cancel pattern:
```javascript
const controller = new AbortController();

try {
  const response = await fetch('/api/data', { signal: controller.signal });
  const json = await response.json();
} catch (err) {
  if (err.name === 'AbortError') {
    console.log('Request was cancelled');
  } else {
    throw err;
  }
}

// Cancel at any time (e.g., user clicks cancel button)
controller.abort();
```

Built-in timeout (no manual controller):
```javascript
const response = await fetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000) });
```

Compose multiple signals — cancel on either:
```javascript
const userController = new AbortController();
const combined = AbortSignal.any([
  userController.signal,
  AbortSignal.timeout(10000)
]);
const response = await fetch(url, { signal: combined });
```

Abortable custom promise:
```javascript
function abortableDelay(ms, signal) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    signal?.throwIfAborted();
    const timer = setTimeout(resolve, ms);
    signal?.addEventListener('abort', () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(signal.reason);
    }, { once: true });
  });
}
```

AbortSignal.aborted (boolean), AbortSignal.reason (the abort reason). After abort, all future fetch() calls with same signal reject immediately.

**Scenario (Finance):** Implementing this pattern to handle transactions efficiently.

