for-await-of iterates async iterables (and sync iterables) inside async contexts. Awaits each value before proceeding to next iteration.

Syntax:
```javascript
for await (const value of asyncIterable) { ... }
```

With async generator:
```javascript
async function* asyncGenerator() {
  let i = 0;
  while (i < 3) yield i++;
}

(async () => {
  for await (const num of asyncGenerator()) {
    console.log(num); // 0, 1, 2
  }
})();
```

With fetch stream:
```javascript
async function* streamLines(response) {
  const reader = response.body.pipeThrough(new TextDecoderStream()).getReader();
  try {
    while (true) {
      const { done, value } = await reader.read();
      if (done) return;
      yield value;
    }
  } finally {
    reader.releaseLock(); // cleanup on break/error
  }
}

const response = await fetch('/large-file.txt');
for await (const line of streamLines(response)) {
  processLine(line);
}
```

Difference from for-of: for-of returns promises as-is; for-await-of resolves them.

Caveat — sync generators yielding rejected promises:
```javascript
// finally blocks NOT called when sync generator yields rejected promise in for-await-of
// Use async generators instead for proper cleanup
```

Break triggers cleanup: calls return() on the iterator, which runs finally blocks in async generators.

