setTimeout schedules a callback after at least delay milliseconds. Executes after current synchronous code and microtasks complete. Returns to event loop before firing.

Event loop interaction:
```javascript
setTimeout(() => console.log('timeout'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('microtask'));
console.log('sync');
// Output: sync → microtask → timeout
// (microtasks always drain before next macrotask)
```

Nested setTimeout throttling: After 5+ levels of nesting, browsers enforce minimum 4ms delay (HTML spec). Inactive tabs throttled to 1000ms+.

Concurrency pitfall — this context loss:
```javascript
class Timer {
  tick() { console.log(this); } // needs 'this'
}
const t = new Timer();
setTimeout(t.tick, 1000); // WRONG: 'this' is undefined/window
setTimeout(() => t.tick(), 1000); // CORRECT: arrow captures 't'
setTimeout(t.tick.bind(t), 1000); // CORRECT: explicit bind
```

Concurrency pitfall — string coercion (silent bug):
```javascript
setTimeout(callback, '1 second'); // delay coerced to NaN → 0 (fires immediately)
```

Recursive setTimeout vs setInterval (prevents overlap):
```javascript
// setInterval: callbacks may queue if execution > interval
// Recursive setTimeout: waits for completion before scheduling next
(function loop() {
  setTimeout(() => {
    doWork();
    loop(); // schedule next AFTER work done
  }, 1000);
})();
```

Max delay: 2,147,483,647ms (~24.8 days). Larger values overflow to 0 or negative (fires immediately).

