Promise.resolve(value) wraps a value in a resolved promise. Promise.reject(reason) wraps a value in a rejected promise.

Promise.resolve behaviors:
```javascript
// Primitives → fulfilled promise
Promise.resolve(42).then(v => console.log(v)); // 42

// Existing promise → same instance returned (no wrapping)
const p = Promise.resolve(42);
console.log(Promise.resolve(p) === p); // true

// Thenable → adopted
Promise.resolve({ then(res) { res('from thenable'); } })
  .then(v => console.log(v)); // 'from thenable'

// Starting a chain from a value
Promise.resolve(userData)
  .then(validate)
  .then(save)
  .catch(handleError);
```

Promise.reject behaviors:
```javascript
// Always creates new promise, even for promise inputs
const orig = Promise.resolve(1);
const rejected = Promise.reject(orig);
console.log(rejected === orig); // false
// orig itself is the rejection reason

// Best practice: reject with Error objects for stack traces
Promise.reject(new Error('Connection failed'))
  .catch(err => console.error(err.stack));

// Fire-and-forget with handled rejection
void fetch('/api').catch(console.error);
```

Note: Promise.resolve() does NOT catch synchronous errors thrown when computing the value. Use Promise.try() for that.

