**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

SharedWorker is accessible by multiple pages from the same origin. Uses port-based communication. Persists as long as any page holds a reference.

Main thread (each page):
```javascript
const worker = new SharedWorker('shared-worker.js');

// Must start port before using addEventListener
worker.port.start();

worker.port.postMessage({ type: 'REQUEST', id: 1 });

worker.port.onmessage = (e) => {
  console.log('Shared worker says:', e.data);
};
```

Shared worker script:
```javascript
// shared-worker.js
const clients = new Set(); // track all connected pages

onconnect = (e) => {
  const port = e.ports[0];
  clients.add(port);

  port.addEventListener('message', (e) => {
    // Broadcast to all connected clients
    for (const client of clients) {
      client.postMessage({ type: 'UPDATE', data: e.data });
    }
  });

  port.addEventListener('close', () => clients.delete(port));

  port.start();
};
```

Use cases: shared WebSocket connection across tabs, centralized cache, cross-tab state synchronization without server. Difference from BroadcastChannel: SharedWorker can compute, maintain state, and filter messages; BroadcastChannel only relays messages.

