**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Classic race condition: a loop creates async callbacks that all close over the same variable — by the time they execute, the variable has the final loop value.

PROBLEM (var):
```javascript
const funcs = [];
for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  funcs.push(setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 100));
}
// Output: 5 5 5 5 5 (all see i=5 after loop ends)
```

PROBLEM (async with shared counter):
```javascript
let count = 0;
const ops = [];
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  ops.push(fetchAndUpdate(i)); // all capture same 'i'
}
await Promise.all(ops);
// i is always 3 in all callbacks
```

FIX 1 — use let (block scope per iteration):
```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 100); // 0 1 2 3 4
}
```

FIX 2 — IIFE to capture per iteration:
```javascript
for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  ((capturedI) => {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(capturedI), 100);
  })(i);
}
```

FIX 3 — pass as parameter:
```javascript
for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  setTimeout((capturedI) => console.log(capturedI), 100, i);
}
```

RELATED — async/await in loop (always use for-of, not for-in with var):
```javascript
const results = await Promise.all(
  items.map(async (item) => processItem(item)) // each captures own item
);
```

