setInterval does not wait for the previous callback to complete. If callback execution time exceeds the interval, callbacks overlap — concurrent execution of the same function.

PROBLEM:
```javascript
let processing = false;

setInterval(async () => {
  // If previous execution hasn't finished, this runs anyway
  const data = await fetchLatestData(); // takes 2000ms
  await saveToDatabase(data);
}, 1000); // interval 1000ms < execution 2000ms
// Two concurrent database writes start — duplicate data, corruption
```

FIX 1 — guard flag (basic):
```javascript
let isProcessing = false;

setInterval(async () => {
  if (isProcessing) return; // skip if already running
  isProcessing = true;
  try {
    const data = await fetchLatestData();
    await saveToDatabase(data);
  } finally {
    isProcessing = false;
  }
}, 1000);
```

FIX 2 — recursive setTimeout (preferred):
```javascript
async function poll() {
  try {
    const data = await fetchLatestData();
    await saveToDatabase(data);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Poll failed:', err);
  } finally {
    setTimeout(poll, 1000); // schedule AFTER completion
  }
}

poll(); // kick off
```

FIX 3 — AbortController to cancel stale operations:
```javascript
let controller = new AbortController();

setInterval(() => {
  controller.abort(); // cancel previous
  controller = new AbortController();
  fetchData({ signal: controller.signal }).then(save).catch(() => {});
}, 1000);
```

