**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Async form submission without disabling the trigger allows multiple concurrent submissions — duplicate records, double-charged payments, duplicate API calls.

PROBLEM:
```javascript
submitBtn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
  // User clicks twice quickly — two concurrent submissions
  const result = await api.submitOrder(orderData);
  showSuccess(result);
});
```

FIX 1 — disable while pending:
```javascript
submitBtn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
  if (submitBtn.disabled) return;
  submitBtn.disabled = true;
  try {
    const result = await api.submitOrder(orderData);
    showSuccess(result);
  } catch (err) {
    showError(err);
  } finally {
    submitBtn.disabled = false; // re-enable
  }
});
```

FIX 2 — promise guard (debounce for async):
```javascript
let pendingSubmit = null;

submitBtn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
  if (pendingSubmit) return; // already in flight
  pendingSubmit = api.submitOrder(orderData);
  try {
    const result = await pendingSubmit;
    showSuccess(result);
  } finally {
    pendingSubmit = null;
  }
});
```

FIX 3 — idempotency key (server-side defense):
```javascript
const requestId = crypto.randomUUID();
await api.submitOrder(orderData, { idempotencyKey: requestId });
// Server deduplicates by idempotencyKey — even if two requests arrive
```

Combine: client-side disable + server-side idempotency for double protection.

