**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Reading and writing SharedArrayBuffer without Atomics causes data races — values may be partially written, reordered by CPU, or invisible to other threads.

PROBLEM — non-atomic increment:
```javascript
// Worker A and Worker B both run simultaneously:
const shared = new Int32Array(sharedBuffer);

// Worker A:
const val = shared[0];  // read: 5
shared[0] = val + 1;    // write: 6

// Worker B (concurrent):
const val = shared[0];  // also reads: 5 (before A wrote)
shared[0] = val + 1;    // writes: 6 (overwrites A's write)

// Expected: 7, Actual: 6 — lost update
```

FIX — use Atomics for all shared access:
```javascript
// Worker A:
Atomics.add(shared, 0, 1); // atomic: read-modify-write in one step

// Worker B:
Atomics.add(shared, 0, 1);

// Result: always 7
```

FIX — CAS for conditional update:
```javascript
function atomicIncrementIfLessThan(arr, index, max) {
  while (true) {
    const current = Atomics.load(arr, index);
    if (current >= max) return false;
    const prev = Atomics.compareExchange(arr, index, current, current + 1);
    if (prev === current) return true; // success
    // prev !== current: another thread changed it — retry
  }
}
```

Wait/notify for producer-consumer:
```javascript
// Consumer worker: wait for data
Atomics.wait(arr, 0, 0); // wait while arr[0] === 0
processData(arr);

// Producer:
writeData(arr);
Atomics.notify(arr, 0, 1); // wake one consumer
```

Atomics.wait() cannot be called on main thread — use Atomics.waitAsync() instead.

