**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Awaiting independent async operations sequentially wastes time. Each await blocks until completion before starting the next, even when operations have no dependency.

PROBLEM — sequential when operations are independent:
```javascript
async function getDashboardData(userId) {
  const user = await fetchUser(userId);         // 200ms
  const orders = await fetchOrders(userId);     // 300ms
  const messages = await fetchMessages(userId); // 150ms
  return { user, orders, messages };
  // Total: ~650ms sequential
}
```

FIX — concurrent with Promise.all:
```javascript
async function getDashboardData(userId) {
  const [user, orders, messages] = await Promise.all([
    fetchUser(userId),
    fetchOrders(userId),
    fetchMessages(userId),
  ]);
  return { user, orders, messages };
  // Total: ~300ms (slowest single request)
}
```

Keep sequential when there is a dependency:
```javascript
// CORRECT — orders depends on user.id
const user = await fetchUser(email);
const orders = await fetchOrders(user.id); // needs user first
```

Common mistake — storing promises then immediately awaiting:
```javascript
// STILL SEQUENTIAL — promise created then immediately awaited
const userP = fetchUser();       // starts
const user = await userP;        // waits
const ordersP = fetchOrders();   // only starts after user done

// CORRECT — start all first, await later
const userP = fetchUser();
const ordersP = fetchOrders();
const [user, orders] = await Promise.all([userP, ordersP]);
```

Rule: Only use sequential await when B genuinely requires A's result. Otherwise, start all operations together and collect with Promise.all.

