**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

A concurrency limiter ensures at most N async operations run simultaneously. Prevents overwhelming external APIs, databases, or file system with too many concurrent requests.

Simple semaphore:
```javascript
class Semaphore {
  constructor(max) {
    this.max = max;
    this.count = 0;
    this.queue = [];
  }

  async acquire() {
    if (this.count < this.max) {
      this.count++;
      return;
    }
    await new Promise(resolve => this.queue.push(resolve));
    this.count++;
  }

  release() {
    this.count--;
    const next = this.queue.shift();
    if (next) next();
  }

  async run(fn) {
    await this.acquire();
    try {
      return await fn();
    } finally {
      this.release();
    }
  }
}

const sem = new Semaphore(3); // max 3 concurrent
const results = await Promise.all(
  urls.map(url => sem.run(() => fetch(url).then(r => r.json())))
);
```

p-limit pattern (lightweight):
```javascript
async function* chunks(arr, size) {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += size) {
    yield arr.slice(i, i + size);
  }
}

async function processWithLimit(items, limit, fn) {
  const results = [];
  for await (const batch of chunks(items, limit)) {
    const batchResults = await Promise.all(batch.map(fn));
    results.push(...batchResults);
  }
  return results;
}

// Process 5 at a time
await processWithLimit(1000Items, 5, processItem);
```

