**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

A promise queue ensures operations execute one at a time (serial) — useful for ordering mutations, preventing interleaving, or enforcing sequential access to a shared resource.

Simple serial queue:
```javascript
class PromiseQueue {
  constructor() {
    this._chain = Promise.resolve();
  }

  enqueue(fn) {
    this._chain = this._chain.then(() => fn());
    return this._chain;
  }
}

const queue = new PromiseQueue();

// All enqueued operations execute in order, one at a time
queue.enqueue(() => saveUser(user1));
queue.enqueue(() => saveUser(user2));
queue.enqueue(() => sendEmail(user1));
// saveUser(user1) → saveUser(user2) → sendEmail(user1) — guaranteed order
```

With error isolation (one failure doesn't break the queue):
```javascript
class ResilientQueue {
  constructor() {
    this._chain = Promise.resolve();
  }

  enqueue(fn) {
    this._chain = this._chain
      .then(() => fn())
      .catch(err => console.error('Queue item failed:', err));
    return this._chain;
  }
}
```

With result collection:
```javascript
const results = [];
const tasks = [task1, task2, task3];
const final = tasks.reduce(
  (chain, task) => chain.then(async () => {
    const result = await task();
    results.push(result);
  }),
  Promise.resolve()
);
await final;
```

Use case: API rate limiting, ordered write operations, animation frame sequences.

