EventEmitter (Node.js built-in, or browser equivalent) provides a publish-subscribe mechanism for decoupled async event propagation.

Node.js EventEmitter:
```javascript
const { EventEmitter } = require('events');

class DataProcessor extends EventEmitter {
  async process(items) {
    for (const item of items) {
      try {
        const result = await processItem(item);
        this.emit('item', result);         // success event
      } catch (err) {
        this.emit('error', err, item);    // error event
      }
    }
    this.emit('done', { total: items.length }); // completion event
  }
}

const processor = new DataProcessor();
processor.on('item', (result) => saveResult(result));
processor.on('error', (err, item) => logFailure(err, item));
processor.on('done', ({ total }) => console.log(`Processed ${total}`));

await processor.process(myItems);
```

Once listener (auto-remove after first event):
```javascript
processor.once('done', cleanup);
```

EventEmitter + async generator bridge:
```javascript
async function* fromEmitter(emitter, eventName) {
  const { promise, resolve } = Promise.withResolvers();
  const items = [];

  emitter.on(eventName, (data) => {
    items.push(data);
    resolve();
  });

  while (true) {
    await promise;
    yield* items.splice(0);
  }
}

for await (const item of fromEmitter(emitter, 'data')) {
  process(item);
}
```

Max listeners default is 10 — increase with setMaxListeners() or use once() to prevent memory leaks from listener accumulation.

