Async code commonly causes memory leaks by keeping references alive longer than needed: closures in long-lived event handlers, abandoned promises, unreleased streams.

Leak 1 — event listeners not removed:
```javascript
// LEAK: 'message' listener keeps worker closure alive
const worker = new Worker('./worker.js');
worker.addEventListener('message', (e) => {
  updateGiantDataStructure(e.data); // data structure never GC'd
});
// worker.terminate() doesn't remove listeners — add removeEventListener

// FIX:
function onMessage(e) { updateGiantDataStructure(e.data); }
worker.addEventListener('message', onMessage);
// Later:
worker.removeEventListener('message', onMessage);
worker.terminate();
```

Leak 2 — promise keeping closure alive:
```javascript
// LEAK: promise chain holds reference to largeData
const largeData = new Uint8Array(1024 * 1024 * 100);
const p = fetch('/api').then(r => {
  process(largeData); // largeData kept in memory until p resolves
  return r.json();
});
// FIX: process then release reference
const largeData = new Uint8Array(...);
const copy = largeData.slice(0, 100); // extract only what's needed
const p = fetch('/api').then(r => { process(copy); return r.json(); });
```

WeakRef for optional caches:
```javascript
const cache = new Map();

function getCached(key, factory) {
  const ref = cache.get(key);
  const existing = ref?.deref();
  if (existing) return existing;

  const value = factory();
  cache.set(key, new WeakRef(value));
  return value;
}
// value can be GC'd; cache entry becomes stale but won't prevent GC
```

Stream cleanup — always cancel/close readable streams when done:
```javascript
const reader = stream.getReader();
try {
  // process stream
} finally {
  reader.cancel();
  reader.releaseLock();
}
```

