**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Fetch data from multiple APIs concurrently, handle partial failures gracefully, and return a merged result with status info for each source.

```javascript
async function aggregateAPIs(sources) {
  const results = await Promise.allSettled(
    sources.map(async ({ name, url, transform }) => {
      const signal = AbortSignal.timeout(5000); // 5s per source
      const res = await fetch(url, { signal });
      if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status} from ${name}`);
      const data = await res.json();
      return { name, data: transform ? transform(data) : data };
    })
  );

  const aggregated = {};
  const errors = [];

  for (const result of results) {
    if (result.status === 'fulfilled') {
      aggregated[result.value.name] = result.value.data;
    } else {
      errors.push(result.reason.message);
    }
  }

  return { data: aggregated, errors, partial: errors.length > 0 };
}

// Usage:
const { data, errors, partial } = await aggregateAPIs([
  { name: 'users', url: '/api/users', transform: r => r.items },
  { name: 'products', url: '/api/products' },
  { name: 'inventory', url: '/api/inventory' },
]);

if (partial) console.warn('Some sources failed:', errors);
renderDashboard(data);
```

Key patterns used: Promise.allSettled (partial failure tolerance), AbortSignal.timeout (per-request timeout), transform functions (normalize data shapes), status tracking (know which sources failed).

