Service Worker is a special worker that acts as a network proxy — intercepts fetch requests, manages caches, handles background sync. Runs in its own thread, survives page close.

Registration:
```javascript
if ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {
  const reg = await navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js', { scope: '/' });
  console.log('SW registered:', reg.scope);
}
```

Service worker lifecycle (sw.js):
```javascript
// Install: cache static assets
self.addEventListener('install', (event) => {
  event.waitUntil(
    caches.open('v1').then(cache =>
      cache.addAll(['/index.html', '/style.css', '/app.js'])
    )
  );
});

// Activate: clean old caches
self.addEventListener('activate', (event) => {
  event.waitUntil(
    caches.keys().then(keys =>
      Promise.all(keys.filter(k => k !== 'v1').map(k => caches.delete(k)))
    )
  );
});

// Fetch: intercept network requests
self.addEventListener('fetch', (event) => {
  event.respondWith(
    caches.match(event.request).then(cached =>
      cached || fetch(event.request)
    )
  );
});
```

Cache-first strategy (offline support):
```javascript
self.addEventListener('fetch', (event) => {
  event.respondWith(
    caches.match(event.request)
      .then(cached => cached ?? fetch(event.request)
        .then(response => {
          const clone = response.clone();
          caches.open('v1').then(c => c.put(event.request, clone));
          return response;
        })
      )
  );
});
```

Difference from Web Worker: Service workers intercept network, have cache API access, don't need a page open. Web workers do computation on behalf of a specific page.

