requestAnimationFrame (rAF) schedules a callback to run before the next browser paint, synchronized to the display refresh rate (60Hz, 120Hz). Pauses automatically in background tabs.

Animation loop pattern:
```javascript
function animate(timestamp) {
  // timestamp is DOMHighResTimeStamp
  updateGameState(timestamp);
  renderFrame();
  requestAnimationFrame(animate); // schedule next frame
}

requestAnimationFrame(animate); // kick off
```

Time-based animation (frame-rate independent):
```javascript
let previousTimestamp;
const element = document.querySelector('#ball');
let position = 0;

function step(timestamp) {
  if (!previousTimestamp) previousTimestamp = timestamp;
  const elapsed = timestamp - previousTimestamp;
  previousTimestamp = timestamp;

  position += 0.1 * elapsed; // pixels per ms
  element.style.transform = `translateX(${position}px)`;

  if (position < 500) requestAnimationFrame(step);
}

requestAnimationFrame(step);
```

Cancellation:
```javascript
const rafId = requestAnimationFrame(animate);
cancelAnimationFrame(rafId);
```

Batch DOM updates to single frame:
```javascript
const updates = [];

function scheduleUpdate(fn) {
  updates.push(fn);
  if (updates.length === 1) {
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      const pending = updates.splice(0);
      pending.forEach(fn => fn());
    });
  }
}

// All DOM changes in one frame — no layout thrashing
scheduleUpdate(() => el1.textContent = newText);
scheduleUpdate(() => el2.style.color = newColor);
```

vs setTimeout: rAF is synchronized to repaint (no tearing, no battery drain). setTimeout fires regardless of display state — wrong timing for animations.

