Iterator helper methods (map, filter, take, drop, flatMap, reduce, forEach, some, every, find, toArray) enable lazy functional pipelines without intermediate arrays.

Chainable lazy pipeline:
```javascript
// Array: creates 3 intermediate arrays
const result = largeArray
  .filter(x => x % 2 === 0)
  .map(x => x * 2)
  .slice(0, 10);

// Iterator: lazy — processes elements one at a time, stops at 10
const result = largeArray
  .values()          // get iterator
  .filter(x => x % 2 === 0)
  .map(x => x * 2)
  .take(10)
  .toArray();
```

With infinite generator:
```javascript
function* naturals() {
  let n = 1;
  while (true) yield n++;
}

// First 5 even squares — stops lazily
const first5EvenSquares = naturals()
  .map(n => n * n)
  .filter(n => n % 2 === 0)
  .take(5)
  .toArray();
// [4, 16, 36, 64, 100]
```

Iterator.from() — wrap any iterable:
```javascript
const mapIter = Iterator.from(myMap.entries())
  .filter(([k, v]) => v > 0)
  .map(([k, v]) => ({ key: k, value: v }))
  .toArray();
```

Iterator.concat() — chain multiple iterables lazily:
```javascript
const merged = Iterator.concat(iter1, iter2, iter3);
```

Iterator.zip() — parallel iteration:
```javascript
const pairs = Iterator.zip(keysIter, valuesIter).toArray();
// [[key1, val1], [key2, val2], ...]
```

Key advantage: no intermediate arrays — constant memory for long/infinite sequences.

