**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Mixing callback-style and promise-style async code leads to unhandled errors, inconsistent control flow, and hard-to-debug timing issues.

PROBLEM — callback inside promise chain:
```javascript
function loadUser(id, callback) {
  fetch(`/api/user/${id}`)
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(user => callback(null, user)) // callback in promise chain
    .catch(err => callback(err));       // errors handled twice?
}

loadUser(1, (err, user) => {
  if (err) throw err; // throw inside callback — UNHANDLED
  updateUI(user);
});
```

PROBLEM — promisified callback with dangling rejection:
```javascript
new Promise((resolve) => {
  fs.readFile('data.txt', (err, data) => {
    if (err) throw err; // ERROR: throws inside sync callback, not caught by Promise
    resolve(data);
  });
});
```

FIX — always reject, never throw in callbacks:
```javascript
new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  fs.readFile('data.txt', (err, data) => {
    if (err) reject(err); // correct
    else resolve(data);
  });
});
```

FIX — promisify consistently:
```javascript
// Use util.promisify (Node.js) or manual wrapper
const readFile = require('fs').promises.readFile;

async function loadConfig() {
  const data = await readFile('config.json', 'utf8');
  return JSON.parse(data);
}

// Never mix — pick one style per function
```

Rule: Never mix async styles in the same function. Convert legacy callback APIs to promises at the boundary, use async/await throughout.

