**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Empty catch blocks or catch blocks that only log silently hide real errors — callers cannot know the operation failed, leading to incorrect application state.

PROBLEM — swallowing with empty catch:
```javascript
async function saveUser(user) {
  try {
    await db.users.insert(user);
  } catch (e) {
    // error silently ignored
  }
  return user; // caller thinks save succeeded
}

const saved = await saveUser(newUser);
// newUser was never saved — caller doesn't know
```

PROBLEM — only logging without re-throwing:
```javascript
async function processOrder(order) {
  try {
    await chargeCard(order.amount);
    await fulfillOrder(order);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Order failed:', err); // logged, but not propagated
  }
  return order; // always returns success — order may be half-processed
}
```

FIX — always re-throw or return sentinel:
```javascript
async function saveUser(user) {
  try {
    await db.users.insert(user);
    return { success: true, user };
  } catch (err) {
    // log for observability
    logger.error('Save failed', { userId: user.id, err });
    // propagate to caller
    throw new Error(`Failed to save user ${user.id}: ${err.message}`);
  }
}
```

FIX — catch specific errors, re-throw unknown:
```javascript
try {
  await riskyOperation();
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof NetworkError) {
    return fallbackValue; // known, recoverable
  }
  throw err; // unknown — let it propagate
}
```

