**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Add a timeout to any promise-based operation using Promise.race with a timer — operation either completes in time or rejects with TimeoutError.

Basic timeout:
```javascript
function withTimeout(promise, ms, errorMessage = 'Operation timed out') {
  const timeout = new Promise((_, reject) =>
    setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(errorMessage)), ms)
  );
  return Promise.race([promise, timeout]);
}

// Usage
try {
  const result = await withTimeout(slowOperation(), 5000);
} catch (err) {
  if (err.message === 'Operation timed out') {
    handleTimeout();
  }
}
```

With AbortController (proper cleanup):
```javascript
function withTimeout(asyncFn, ms) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timer = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(new Error('Timeout')), ms);

  return asyncFn(controller.signal)
    .finally(() => clearTimeout(timer));
}

// Operation must accept signal
const data = await withTimeout(
  (signal) => fetch('/api/slow', { signal }).then(r => r.json()),
  5000
);
```

Simplest (AbortSignal.timeout built-in):
```javascript
const result = await fetch('/api/data', {
  signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000)
}).then(r => r.json());
```

Timeout for non-fetch operations:
```javascript
async function withAbortTimeout(fn, ms) {
  const { promise, resolve, reject } = Promise.withResolvers();
  const timer = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(`Timed out after ${ms}ms`)), ms);

  try {
    resolve(await fn());
  } catch (err) {
    reject(err);
  } finally {
    clearTimeout(timer);
  }

  return promise;
}
```

