**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Marking every function async and awaiting everything sequentially — including synchronous operations — adds unnecessary microtask overhead and serializes work that could be parallel.

PROBLEM — async where not needed:
```javascript
// These are synchronous — async adds overhead for no benefit
async function add(a, b) { return a + b; }
async function formatName(first, last) { return `${first} ${last}`; }

async function processUser(user) {
  const sum = await add(1, 2);           // needless await
  const name = await formatName(user.first, user.last); // needless await
  return { sum, name };
}
```

PROBLEM — serializing independent operations:
```javascript
async function buildReport(userId) {
  const profile = await fetchProfile(userId);  // 200ms
  const orders = await fetchOrders(userId);    // 300ms
  const metrics = await fetchMetrics(userId);  // 150ms
  return { profile, orders, metrics };
  // Total: 650ms — all could run in parallel
}
```

FIX — only async where needed:
```javascript
// Synchronous functions stay synchronous
function add(a, b) { return a + b; }
function formatName(first, last) { return `${first} ${last}`; }

async function buildReport(userId) {
  const [profile, orders, metrics] = await Promise.all([
    fetchProfile(userId),
    fetchOrders(userId),
    fetchMetrics(userId),
  ]);
  // Total: ~300ms

  const name = formatName(profile.first, profile.last); // sync, no await
  return { profile, orders, metrics, name };
}
```

FIX — avoid async wrapping of sync utilities:
```javascript
// WRONG
async function parseConfig(json) { return JSON.parse(json); }

// RIGHT
function parseConfig(json) { return JSON.parse(json); }

// Wrap in Promise.try only when caller needs uniform async handling
const config = await Promise.try(() => parseConfig(rawJson));
```

