**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Array.from() creates arrays from iterables/array-like objects with optional mapping. Useful pattern for spawning fixed-size worker pools and generating task batches for concurrent processing.

Create N workers:
```javascript
const WORKER_COUNT = navigator.hardwareConcurrency || 4;

const workers = Array.from(
  { length: WORKER_COUNT },
  () => new Worker('./compute-worker.js')
);
// [Worker, Worker, Worker, Worker]
```

Generate parallel task ranges:
```javascript
function splitForParallel(totalItems, workerCount) {
  const chunkSize = Math.ceil(totalItems / workerCount);
  return Array.from({ length: workerCount }, (_, i) => ({
    start: i * chunkSize,
    end: Math.min((i + 1) * chunkSize, totalItems),
    workerId: i,
  }));
}

const ranges = splitForParallel(1000000, 4);
// [{start:0, end:250000}, {start:250000, end:500000}, ...]

const results = await Promise.all(
  ranges.map((range, i) => runWorker(workers[i], range))
);
```

Array.fromAsync() — for async iterables:
```javascript
const pages = await Array.fromAsync(
  fetchAllPages('/api/items') // async generator
);
// Collects all yielded pages into array
```

Convert Map/Set to array for concurrent processing:
```javascript
const results = await Promise.all(
  Array.from(pendingRequests.values(), req => processRequest(req))
);
```

