The Streams API enables processing data in chunks as it arrives. Three stream types: ReadableStream (source), WritableStream (sink), TransformStream (processor). Backpressure prevents fast producers from overwhelming slow consumers.

Stream pipeline architecture:
```
ReadableStream → TransformStream → WritableStream
     ↑               ↑                  ↑
  source         process           destination
```

Backpressure — consumer signals producer to slow down:
```javascript
const writableStream = new WritableStream({
  write(chunk, controller) {
    // If this returns a pending promise, WritableStream applies backpressure
    return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 10)); // slow consumer
  }
});

// pipeTo() automatically handles backpressure — pauses ReadableStream
await fastReadableStream.pipeTo(writableStream);
```

Custom readable with pull-based backpressure:
```javascript
const readable = new ReadableStream({
  async pull(controller) {
    // Called by runtime only when consumer wants more data
    const chunk = await fetchNextChunk();
    if (chunk) controller.enqueue(chunk);
    else controller.close();
  },
  cancel(reason) {
    console.log('Consumer cancelled:', reason);
  }
}, new CountQueuingStrategy({ highWaterMark: 3 })); // max 3 buffered chunks
```

Tee — fan-out one stream to two consumers:
```javascript
const [forCache, forUI] = response.body.tee();
await Promise.all([
  forCache.pipeTo(cacheWriter),
  forUI.pipeThrough(new TextDecoderStream()).pipeTo(uiWriter),
]);
```

Locking: once a reader/writer is acquired, stream is locked to that accessor — prevents concurrent access.

