WeakRef holds a weak reference to an object — does not prevent garbage collection. Use in caches where stale entries should be automatically reclaimed.

Basic usage:
```javascript
const weakRef = new WeakRef(myObject);

// Later: deref() returns object or undefined if GC'd
const obj = weakRef.deref();
if (obj) {
  useObject(obj);
} else {
  // Object was garbage collected
}
```

Async cache with automatic cleanup:
```javascript
const computedCache = new Map();

function cachedCompute(key, factory) {
  const ref = computedCache.get(key);
  const existing = ref?.deref();
  if (existing !== undefined) return existing;

  const result = factory();
  computedCache.set(key, new WeakRef(result));
  return result;
}

// result can be GC'd when no other code holds it
// cache entry becomes stale but doesn't pin result in memory
```

With DOM elements (auto-cleanup when element removed):
```javascript
class ElementTracker {
  constructor(element) {
    this.ref = new WeakRef(element);
    this.intervalId = setInterval(() => {
      const el = this.ref.deref();
      if (el) {
        el.textContent = `Updated: ${Date.now()}`;
      } else {
        clearInterval(this.intervalId); // element removed from DOM, stop
      }
    }, 1000);
  }
}
```

Limitations: GC timing non-deterministic — deref() may return the object even without strong references. Never rely on specific GC timing. Use WeakRef for optimization, not correctness.

