FinalizationRegistry registers a cleanup callback to run after an object is garbage collected. Useful for evicting cache entries and releasing associated resources when tracked objects are GC'd.

```javascript
const registry = new FinalizationRegistry((key) => {
  // Called when registered object is GC'd
  console.log(`Object for key ${key} was collected`);
  cache.delete(key); // evict stale cache entry
});

// Use target itself as unregister token
function cacheWithEviction(key, factory) {
  const value = factory();
  cache.set(key, value);
  registry.register(value, key, value); // (target, heldValue, token)
  return value;
}

// If value is GC'd, cache entry is cleaned up automatically
```

With WeakRef for optional cache:
```javascript
const cache = new Map();
const evictionRegistry = new FinalizationRegistry((cacheKey) => {
  cache.delete(cacheKey);
});

function getMemoized(key, compute) {
  const ref = cache.get(key);
  const cached = ref?.deref();

  if (cached !== undefined) return cached;

  const result = compute();
  cache.set(key, new WeakRef(result));
  evictionRegistry.register(result, key, result);
  return result;
}
```

Critical limitations:
- Callback may NEVER fire (implementation-dependent)
- Timing is non-deterministic
- Never use for essential program logic — only optional cleanup
- Program shutdown may skip all pending callbacks

Prefer WeakMap/WeakSet when possible — they handle lifetime-coupled metadata without callback uncertainty.

