**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Map is the preferred data structure for tracking concurrent async operations — O(1) size, any key type, guaranteed insertion order, no prototype pollution.

In-flight request registry:
```javascript
class RequestRegistry {
  constructor() {
    this.inFlight = new Map(); // requestId → { promise, startTime }
  }

  register(id, promise) {
    this.inFlight.set(id, { promise, startTime: Date.now() });
    promise.finally(() => this.inFlight.delete(id));
    return promise;
  }

  isInFlight(id) { return this.inFlight.has(id); }
  count() { return this.inFlight.size; } // O(1)

  async getOrFetch(id, fetcher) {
    if (this.inFlight.has(id)) {
      return this.inFlight.get(id).promise; // reuse existing
    }
    return this.register(id, fetcher());
  }
}

const registry = new RequestRegistry();

// Concurrent calls for same ID — only one fetch
const [r1, r2, r3] = await Promise.all([
  registry.getOrFetch('user-1', () => fetch('/api/user/1').then(r => r.json())),
  registry.getOrFetch('user-1', () => fetch('/api/user/1').then(r => r.json())),
  registry.getOrFetch('user-1', () => fetch('/api/user/1').then(r => r.json())),
]);
// r1 === r2 === r3 — all await same promise
```

Map vs Object for concurrent state:
- Map: `map.size` O(1), any key type, no __proto__ risk
- Object: needs Object.keys().length O(n), string keys only, prototype chain collisions

