Multiple async operations reading and writing the same array without coordination produce non-deterministic results — lost updates, duplicates, wrong order.

PROBLEM — concurrent push to shared array:
```javascript
const results = [];

await Promise.all(items.map(async (item) => {
  const result = await processItem(item);
  results.push(result); // race: multiple pushes interleaved
}));

// results.length === items.length ✓ (JS is single-threaded per task)
// results order is NON-DETERMINISTIC ✗ (depends on which resolves first)
```

PROBLEM — concurrent splice:
```javascript
const queue = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

await Promise.all([
  (async () => {
    const item = queue.splice(0, 1)[0]; // may conflict
    await process(item);
  })(),
  (async () => {
    const item = queue.splice(0, 1)[0]; // splices before first returns
    await process(item);
  })(),
]);
// Both might get item 1 and skip item 2
```

FIX — collect then assign (preserves order):
```javascript
const results = await Promise.all(items.map(processItem));
// results[i] === processItem(items[i]) — guaranteed order
```

FIX — index-based assignment (preserves order, safe for concurrent writes):
```javascript
const results = new Array(items.length);
await Promise.all(
  items.map(async (item, i) => {
    results[i] = await processItem(item); // different index each time
  })
);
// results[i] corresponds to items[i] — deterministic
```

Note: JavaScript is single-threaded — no true concurrent array corruption. The risk is order non-determinism and logic errors, not memory corruption.

