**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Read-modify-write sequences are non-atomic in async code — another operation can modify shared state between the read and the write.

PROBLEM — bank account:
```javascript
let balance = 100;

async function withdraw(amount) {
  const current = balance;  // read: 100
  await validateWithBank(amount); // async — yields control
  // Another withdraw() may have run here and changed balance
  balance = current - amount; // write with stale value
}

await Promise.all([
  withdraw(70), // reads 100, writes 30
  withdraw(70), // also reads 100 (before first write), also writes 30
]);
// balance = 30 — 70 was withdrawn twice without detection
```

FIX — mutex:
```javascript
const balanceMutex = new PromiseQueue();

async function withdraw(amount) {
  return balanceMutex.enqueue(async () => {
    if (balance < amount) throw new Error('Insufficient funds');
    const current = balance;
    await validateWithBank(amount);
    balance = current - amount; // safe — serialized by mutex
    return balance;
  });
}
```

FIX — check-then-act as atomic unit:
```javascript
async function compareAndSwap(expected, newValue) {
  // Entire operation in one microtask — no yield
  if (balance !== expected) return false;
  balance = newValue;
  return true; // success
}

// Retry until CAS succeeds
async function withdraw(amount) {
  while (true) {
    const current = balance;
    if (current < amount) throw new Error('Insufficient');
    if (await compareAndSwap(current, current - amount)) break;
  }
}
```

