EventSource implements Server-Sent Events — persistent HTTP connection where the server pushes data to the client. Auto-reconnects on disconnect. Simpler than WebSocket for server-to-client-only use cases.

Basic usage:
```javascript
const sse = new EventSource('/api/stream');

// Unnamed events
sse.onmessage = (e) => console.log('Data:', e.data);

// Named events (server sends: event: update)
sse.addEventListener('update', (e) => {
  const data = JSON.parse(e.data);
  updateUI(data);
});

sse.onerror = (e) => {
  if (sse.readyState === EventSource.CLOSED) {
    console.log('Connection closed permanently');
  }
};

// Close when done
sse.close();
```

With credentials (cross-origin):
```javascript
const sse = new EventSource('/api/stream', { withCredentials: true });
```

Async iterator bridge:
```javascript
async function* fromSSE(url) {
  const { promise, resolve, reject } = Promise.withResolvers();
  const sse = new EventSource(url);
  const queue = [];

  sse.onmessage = (e) => {
    queue.push(e.data);
    resolve();
  };
  sse.onerror = reject;

  try {
    while (true) {
      await promise;
      yield* queue.splice(0);
    }
  } finally {
    sse.close();
  }
}

for await (const data of fromSSE('/api/stream')) {
  processUpdate(data);
}
```

Concurrency limit: HTTP/1.1 allows only 6 SSE connections per domain across all tabs. Use HTTP/2 (100 concurrent streams) to bypass this limit.

