Array.fromAsync() creates an array by consuming an async iterable — awaiting each value in sequence. Returns a Promise<Array>.

From async generator:
```javascript
async function* paginate(url) {
  let page = 1;
  while (true) {
    const res = await fetch(`${url}?page=${page}`);
    const { items, hasMore } = await res.json();
    yield* items;
    if (!hasMore) break;
    page++;
  }
}

const allItems = await Array.fromAsync(paginate('/api/items'));
```

From set of promises:
```javascript
const promises = new Set([
  Promise.resolve(1),
  Promise.resolve(2),
  Promise.resolve(3),
]);
const values = await Array.fromAsync(promises);
// [1, 2, 3] — awaited in order
```

With map function:
```javascript
const doubled = await Array.fromAsync(
  asyncNumberGenerator(),
  async (n) => n * 2
);
```

Sequential vs concurrent:
```javascript
// Array.fromAsync — SEQUENTIAL (waits for each before next)
const seq = await Array.fromAsync(
  [fetchA(), fetchB(), fetchC()]
);
// Total time: A + B + C

// Promise.all — CONCURRENT (all in flight)
const conc = await Promise.all([fetchA(), fetchB(), fetchC()]);
// Total time: max(A, B, C)
```

Use Array.fromAsync when: you need all items from an async source in an array, source produces items sequentially (pagination, stream). Use Promise.all when: you have all promises upfront and can run them concurrently.

