Mutating shared state objects directly in async code causes race conditions. Use immutable update patterns — create new objects instead of modifying existing ones.

Problematic mutation:
```javascript
const state = { count: 0, items: [], loading: false };

async function loadItems() {
  state.loading = true; // mutation — risky if concurrent
  const items = await fetch('/api/items').then(r => r.json());
  state.items = items; // another mutation
  state.loading = false;
  state.count = items.length;
}
```

Immutable update (spread):
```javascript
let state = { count: 0, items: [], loading: false };

async function loadItems() {
  state = { ...state, loading: true }; // new object
  const items = await fetch('/api/items').then(r => r.json());
  state = { ...state, items, count: items.length, loading: false };
  render(state);
}
```

Atom pattern — versioned state:
```javascript
let version = 0;
let state = {};

async function update(patchFn) {
  const myVersion = ++version;
  const newState = await patchFn(state);

  if (myVersion !== version) return; // stale — newer update ran
  state = { ...state, ...newState };
  render(state);
}
```

Object.assign for merge:
```javascript
// Shallow merge — use for flat state
const newState = Object.assign({}, state, { loading: true });

// Spread is equivalent and preferred for readability
const newState2 = { ...state, loading: true };
```

Deep clone when needed:
```javascript
// For deeply nested state
const newState = structuredClone(state);
newState.nested.value = 42;
```

