Awaiting inside a for loop serializes all iterations — each must complete before the next begins. Almost always wrong when iterations are independent.

PROBLEM — sequential where parallel possible:
```javascript
// SLOW: 1000ms + 1000ms + 1000ms = 3000ms
async function downloadAll(urls) {
  const results = [];
  for (const url of urls) {
    const data = await fetch(url).then(r => r.json()); // sequential
    results.push(data);
  }
  return results;
}
```

FIX — Promise.all + map (all concurrent):
```javascript
// FAST: max(1000ms, 1000ms, 1000ms) = ~1000ms
async function downloadAll(urls) {
  return Promise.all(urls.map(url => fetch(url).then(r => r.json())));
}
```

FIX — with concurrency limit (semaphore):
```javascript
// Controlled: 3 at a time
async function downloadWithLimit(urls, limit = 3) {
  const results = new Array(urls.length);
  const sem = new Semaphore(limit);

  await Promise.all(
    urls.map((url, i) => sem.run(async () => {
      results[i] = await fetch(url).then(r => r.json());
    }))
  );

  return results;
}
```

WHEN sequential loop IS correct:
```javascript
// CORRECT — each iteration depends on previous result
for (const step of migrationSteps) {
  await step.run(currentSchema); // must complete before next
}

// CORRECT — processing events that must be handled in order
for await (const event of eventStream) {
  await handleEvent(event); // ordered processing required
}
```

Rule: Await in loop = intentional serialization. If iterations are independent, use Promise.all.

