**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

When using AbortController to cancel fetch/async operations, not checking for AbortError causes misleading error handling — treating cancellation as a real failure.

PROBLEM — treating abort as error:
```javascript
async function searchItems(query, signal) {
  try {
    const results = await fetch(`/api/search?q=${query}`, { signal }).then(r => r.json());
    renderResults(results);
  } catch (err) {
    // AbortError treated as failure — shows wrong error to user
    showErrorMessage('Search failed. Try again.');
    reportError(err); // floods error monitoring with expected aborts
  }
}
```

FIX — check for AbortError specifically:
```javascript
async function searchItems(query, signal) {
  try {
    const results = await fetch(`/api/search?q=${query}`, { signal }).then(r => r.json());
    renderResults(results);
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === 'AbortError') {
      // Expected cancellation — not an error
      return;
    }
    showErrorMessage('Search failed. Try again.');
    reportError(err);
  }
}
```

FIX — signal.throwIfAborted() for manual checks:
```javascript
async function longOperation(items, signal) {
  for (const item of items) {
    signal.throwIfAborted(); // throws AbortError if aborted
    await processItem(item);
  }
}

try {
  await longOperation(items, controller.signal);
} catch (err) {
  if (err.name !== 'AbortError') throw err;
}
```

FIX — re-throw AbortError to propagate cancellation:
```javascript
async function composed(signal) {
  try {
    return await innerOperation(signal);
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === 'AbortError') throw err; // propagate
    handleOtherError(err);
  }
}
```

