Multiple awaited promises or microtasks do not resolve in a deterministic order when running concurrently — code that assumes they do is fragile.

PROBLEM — assuming first fulfilled = first started:
```javascript
async function wrongAssumption() {
  const promises = [
    fetch('/api/slow'),   // 1000ms
    fetch('/api/fast'),   // 100ms
    fetch('/api/medium'), // 500ms
  ];

  // Promise.race gives FIRST SETTLED — not first started
  const first = await Promise.race(promises);
  // first is /api/fast (100ms), not /api/slow (started first)
}
```

PROBLEM — ordering concurrent results:
```javascript
// WRONG: order depends on which resolves first
const results = [];
await Promise.all([
  fetch('/a').then(r => r.json()).then(data => results.push(data)),
  fetch('/b').then(r => r.json()).then(data => results.push(data)),
]);
// results order is non-deterministic
```

FIX — Promise.all preserves order (index order, not resolution order):
```javascript
// CORRECT: results[0] = /a response, results[1] = /b response, always
const [dataA, dataB] = await Promise.all([
  fetch('/a').then(r => r.json()),
  fetch('/b').then(r => r.json()),
]);
```

FIX — index-based collection for order guarantee:
```javascript
const urls = ['/a', '/b', '/c'];
const results = new Array(urls.length);

await Promise.all(
  urls.map(async (url, i) => {
    results[i] = await fetch(url).then(r => r.json());
  })
);
// results[i] always corresponds to urls[i]
```

FIX — allSettled with ordering:
```javascript
const settled = await Promise.allSettled(promises);
// settled[i] corresponds to promises[i] — guaranteed by spec
```

