OffscreenCanvas decouples canvas rendering from the DOM, enabling heavy graphics computation in Web Workers without blocking the main thread. It is a transferable object — ownership is passed to the worker with zero-copy semantics.

Main thread transfers control:
```javascript
const htmlCanvas = document.getElementById('canvas');
const offscreen = htmlCanvas.transferControlToOffscreen();
const worker = new Worker('renderer.js');
worker.postMessage({ canvas: offscreen }, [offscreen]); // transfer ownership
```

Worker thread renders independently:
```javascript
// renderer.js
self.onmessage = (evt) => {
  const canvas = evt.data.canvas;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

  function render(time) {
    ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
    ctx.fillStyle = `hsl(${time / 10 % 360}, 70%, 50%)`;
    ctx.fillRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
    requestAnimationFrame(render);
  }
  requestAnimationFrame(render);
};
```

Creating OffscreenCanvas directly (no HTML element):
```javascript
const offscreen = new OffscreenCanvas(256, 256);
const gl = offscreen.getContext('webgl');
// ... heavy WebGL computation ...
const bitmap = offscreen.transferToImageBitmap();
// Post bitmap back to main thread for display
self.postMessage({ bitmap }, [bitmap]);
```

Main thread receives and renders bitmap:
```javascript
worker.onmessage = (evt) => {
  const ctx = visibleCanvas.getContext('bitmaprenderer');
  ctx.transferFromImageBitmap(evt.data.bitmap);
};
```

Key benefit: main thread stays responsive for user input while compute-intensive rendering runs in worker. Supports 2d, webgl, webgl2 contexts. Available since March 2023 across all major browsers.

