JavaScript Proxy intercepts object operations via handler traps. The set trap enables reactive patterns — trigger side effects (UI updates, cache invalidation) whenever state mutates, including in async contexts.

Basic reactive state:
```javascript
function createReactiveState(initialState, onChange) {
  return new Proxy(initialState, {
    set(target, prop, value) {
      const old = target[prop];
      target[prop] = value;
      if (old !== value) onChange(prop, value, old);
      return true; // must return true to indicate success
    },
    deleteProperty(target, prop) {
      delete target[prop];
      onChange(prop, undefined);
      return true;
    }
  });
}

const state = createReactiveState({ count: 0, loading: false }, (key, val) => {
  console.log(`State.${key} changed to:`, val);
  renderUI(state);
});

state.loading = true;  // triggers onChange
await fetchData();
state.count += 1;      // triggers onChange
state.loading = false; // triggers onChange
```

Async-safe proxy with validation:
```javascript
const apiState = new Proxy({}, {
  set(target, prop, value) {
    if (prop === 'status' && !['idle','loading','error','success'].includes(value)) {
      throw new TypeError(`Invalid status: ${value}`);
    }
    target[prop] = value;
    return true;
  },
  get(target, prop, receiver) {
    if (prop === 'isLoading') return target.status === 'loading';
    return Reflect.get(target, prop, receiver);
  }
});
```

Deep proxy (recursive):
```javascript
function deepProxy(obj, onChange) {
  return new Proxy(obj, {
    set(target, prop, value) {
      target[prop] = typeof value === 'object' && value !== null
        ? deepProxy(value, onChange)
        : value;
      onChange();
      return true;
    }
  });
}
```

Important: use Reflect.get/set inside traps to avoid infinite recursion. Proxy cannot access private class fields (#name) directly.

