Structured concurrency ensures child tasks cannot outlive their parent scope. All concurrent tasks start and finish within a well-defined block, preventing resource leaks and dangling operations.

Problem — unstructured (fire-and-forget leaks):
```javascript
async function processRequest(req) {
  // These tasks outlive the function — no guarantee they complete
  sendAnalytics(req); // fire and forget
  updateCache(req);   // fire and forget
  return buildResponse(req);
} // function returns, but tasks still running — resource leak
```

Structured approach — all tasks must complete:
```javascript
async function processRequest(req) {
  // All tasks contained within this scope
  const [response] = await Promise.all([
    buildResponse(req),
    sendAnalytics(req),
    updateCache(req)
  ]);
  return response;
} // nothing leaks past this point
```

Task scope with cancellation:
```javascript
async function taskScope(signal, ...tasks) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const childSignal = AbortSignal.any([signal, controller.signal]);

  try {
    return await Promise.all(
      tasks.map(task => task(childSignal))
    );
  } finally {
    controller.abort(); // ensure all tasks cancelled on exit
  }
}

// Usage
await taskScope(parentSignal,
  (sig) => fetchUser(userId, sig),
  (sig) => fetchPosts(userId, sig),
  (sig) => recordVisit(userId, sig)
);
```

Error isolation — one task fails, cancel siblings:
```javascript
async function raceWithCleanup(tasks, signal) {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const combined = AbortSignal.any([signal, controller.signal]);

  try {
    return await Promise.race(
      tasks.map(async (task) => {
        const result = await task(combined);
        controller.abort(); // winner cancels others
        return result;
      })
    );
  } finally {
    controller.abort();
  }
}
```

Rule: every async task must be awaited before its parent scope exits. Use Promise.all, not individual awaits in sequence, for parallel tasks.

