JavaScript constructors are synchronous and cannot await. Common patterns to handle async initialization in classes.

Static factory method (recommended):
```javascript
class DatabasePool {
  #pool;

  constructor(pool) {
    this.#pool = pool; // already initialized
  }

  static async create(config) {
    const pool = await createConnectionPool(config);
    return new DatabasePool(pool);
  }

  async query(sql) {
    const conn = await this.#pool.acquire();
    try {
      return await conn.execute(sql);
    } finally {
      this.#pool.release(conn);
    }
  }
}

// Usage — must await creation
const db = await DatabasePool.create({ host: 'localhost', size: 10 });
const rows = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users');
```

Lazy initialization (init on first use):
```javascript
class LazyService {
  #initPromise = null;
  #client = null;

  async #ensureInit() {
    if (!this.#initPromise) {
      this.#initPromise = this.#doInit();
    }
    await this.#initPromise;
  }

  async #doInit() {
    this.#client = await connectToService();
  }

  async getData() {
    await this.#ensureInit();
    return this.#client.fetch();
  }
}

// No await needed — auto-initializes on first call
const svc = new LazyService();
await svc.getData(); // init happens here
await svc.getData(); // reuses existing connection
```

Anti-pattern — async constructor workaround:
```javascript
// BAD: constructor returns a promise — confusing, breaks instanceof
class BadClass {
  constructor() {
    return (async () => {
      await initialize();
      return this;
    })();
  }
}
```

Disposable async resource (using Symbol.asyncDispose):
```javascript
class ManagedConnection {
  static async create() {
    const conn = new ManagedConnection();
    conn.#handle = await openConnection();
    return conn;
  }
  async [Symbol.asyncDispose]() {
    await this.#handle.close();
  }
}

await using conn = await ManagedConnection.create();
// conn auto-closes at end of block
```

