**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Priority inversion occurs when a high-priority task is blocked waiting for a lower-priority task holding a shared resource. In async JS, this can happen with naive FIFO queues.

Scenario:
```javascript
const queue = [];

// Low-priority background sync added first
queue.push({ priority: 'low', task: syncBackgroundData });

// High-priority user action added after
queue.push({ priority: 'high', task: saveUserInput });

// Naive FIFO processor — high priority has to wait for low-priority
async function processQueue() {
  for (const item of queue) {
    await item.task(); // low-priority blocks high-priority
  }
}
```

Fix — priority queue:
```javascript
class PriorityAsyncQueue {
  #queues = { high: [], medium: [], low: [] };
  #running = false;

  enqueue(priority, task) {
    this.#queues[priority].push(task);
    if (!this.#running) this.#drain();
  }

  async #drain() {
    this.#running = true;
    while (true) {
      const task = this.#nextTask();
      if (!task) break;
      await task();
    }
    this.#running = false;
  }

  #nextTask() {
    for (const priority of ['high', 'medium', 'low']) {
      if (this.#queues[priority].length) {
        return this.#queues[priority].shift();
      }
    }
    return null;
  }
}

const q = new PriorityAsyncQueue();
q.enqueue('low', syncBackgroundData);
q.enqueue('high', saveUserInput); // runs first despite being added second
```

Preemption approach — abort lower-priority tasks:
```javascript
async function preemptableFetch(url, priority) {
  if (priority === 'high') {
    // Abort any pending low-priority requests
    lowPriorityController.abort();
    lowPriorityController = new AbortController();
  }
  return fetch(url, { signal: priority === 'low' ? lowPriorityController.signal : undefined });
}
```

