**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

When using Promise.all or Promise.allSettled, unhandled rejections in non-awaited branches cause silent failures or uncaught exception events.

Silent failure:
```javascript
async function processAll(items) {
  // If any item fails, Promise.all rejects — but we don't await it!
  Promise.all(items.map(processItem)); // fire-and-forget — rejection silently lost
  return 'done';
}
```

Node.js unhandledRejection:
```javascript
// Creates UnhandledPromiseRejectionWarning in Node.js
async function badFlow() {
  const p1 = fetch('/api/fast');
  const p2 = fetch('/api/will-fail'); // rejection pending
  const result = await p1; // only await p1
  return result; // p2 rejection becomes unhandled
}
```

Fix — always await or attach .catch():
```javascript
async function safeProcessAll(items) {
  const results = await Promise.allSettled(items.map(processItem));
  const failures = results.filter(r => r.status === 'rejected');
  if (failures.length) {
    console.error('Some items failed:', failures.map(f => f.reason));
  }
  return results.filter(r => r.status === 'fulfilled').map(r => r.value);
}

// For fire-and-forget: explicit error handling
function safeFireAndForget(fn) {
  fn().catch(err => console.error('Background task failed:', err));
}

// Parallel with error isolation:
async function parallelSafe(tasks) {
  const results = await Promise.allSettled(tasks.map(t => t()));
  return {
    succeeded: results.filter(r => r.status === 'fulfilled').map(r => r.value),
    failed: results.filter(r => r.status === 'rejected').map(r => r.reason)
  };
}
```

Global handler (last resort — don't rely on this):
```javascript
process.on('unhandledRejection', (reason, promise) => {
  console.error('Unhandled rejection at:', promise, 'reason:', reason);
  process.exit(1); // fail fast
});
```

