Scheduler.yield() yields execution to the browser during long tasks, allowing the event loop to process higher-priority work (user input, rendering) before resuming. Better than setTimeout for task splitting because it preserves priority context.

Basic yield during heavy loop:
```javascript
async function processLargeList(items) {
  for (const item of items) {
    processItem(item);
    await scheduler.yield(); // yield after each item — browser can handle clicks, renders
  }
}
```

Chunked processing — yield every N items:
```javascript
async function processInChunks(items, chunkSize = 50) {
  for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
    processItem(items[i]);
    if (i % chunkSize === 0) {
      await scheduler.yield(); // yield every 50 items
    }
  }
}
```

Event handler with immediate feedback:
```javascript
button.addEventListener('click', async () => {
  showSpinner(); // synchronous — user sees immediately
  await scheduler.yield(); // yield so browser can render spinner
  const result = await heavyComputation();
  hideSpinner();
  renderResult(result);
});
```

Priority inheritance — yield inside postTask inherits parent priority:
```javascript
await scheduler.postTask(async () => {
  doFirstPart();
  await scheduler.yield(); // inherits 'background' priority — stays low priority
  doSecondPart();
}, { priority: 'background' });
```

Key differences from setTimeout(fn, 0):
- yield() places continuation at front of its priority queue (faster resume)
- yield() inherits abort signal from parent postTask
- setTimeout has minimum ~4ms delay; yield() resumes as soon as queue allows

Fallback for older browsers:
```javascript
const yieldToMain = () => {
  if ('scheduler' in globalThis && 'yield' in scheduler) {
    return scheduler.yield();
  }
  return new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 0));
};
```

