Bad — sequential (3 seconds total if each takes 1s):
```javascript
async function loadDashboard(userId) {
  const user = await fetchUser(userId);     // 1s
  const orders = await fetchOrders(userId); // 1s (waits for user)
  const prefs = await fetchPrefs(userId);   // 1s (waits for orders)
  return { user, orders, prefs };
}
```

Good — parallel (1 second total):
```javascript
async function loadDashboard(userId) {
  const [user, orders, prefs] = await Promise.all([
    fetchUser(userId),
    fetchOrders(userId),
    fetchPrefs(userId),
  ]);
  return { user, orders, prefs };
}
```

When sequential is correct — dependent calls:
```javascript
async function loadUserPosts(userId) {
  const user = await fetchUser(userId);         // must get user first
  const posts = await fetchPosts(user.blogId);  // depends on user.blogId
  return { user, posts };
}
```

Mixed — some parallel, some dependent:
```javascript
async function loadProfile(userId) {
  const user = await fetchUser(userId); // depends on nothing

  const [posts, followers] = await Promise.all([
    fetchPosts(user.blogId),      // depends on user
    fetchFollowers(user.id),      // depends on user
    // both independent of each other — run parallel
  ]);

  return { user, posts, followers };
}
```

Partial parallelism — split into independent groups:
```javascript
async function loadData() {
  const [config, session] = await Promise.all([
    fetchConfig(),  // independent
    fetchSession(), // independent
  ]);

  // config and session needed before these
  const [data, permissions] = await Promise.all([
    fetchData(config.apiUrl),
    fetchPermissions(session.userId),
  ]);

  return { config, session, data, permissions };
}
```

