**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

A single async function doing too many sequential things — multiple awaits for unrelated operations — is slow, hard to test, and hard to cancel partially.

Bad — monolithic async function:
```javascript
async function initializeApp() {
  const config = await fetchConfig();       // step 1
  const user = await authenticate();       // step 2 (unrelated to config)
  const features = await fetchFeatures();  // step 3 (unrelated to both)
  const analytics = await loadAnalytics(); // step 4 (unrelated)
  setupUI(config, user, features);
  reportAnalytics(analytics);
}
// Total time: sum of all latencies
// Cannot cancel partially
// If step 3 fails, steps 1,2 already consumed resources
```

Refactored — parallel + separated concerns:
```javascript
async function initializeApp(signal) {
  // All independent — run parallel
  const [config, user, features, analytics] = await Promise.all([
    fetchConfig(signal),
    authenticate(signal),
    fetchFeatures(signal),
    loadAnalytics(signal),
  ]);

  setupUI(config, user, features);
  reportAnalytics(analytics);
}
// Total time: max(all latencies) — much faster
```

Progressively parallel — show UI as each resolves:
```javascript
async function initializeApp() {
  const configPromise = fetchConfig();
  const userPromise = authenticate();

  // Show critical path first
  const config = await configPromise;
  applyConfig(config); // render immediately

  const user = await userPromise;
  renderUser(user); // render as soon as available

  // Load non-critical in background
  fetchFeatures().then(setupFeatures);
  loadAnalytics().then(reportAnalytics);
}
```

Split by concern:
```javascript
const [criticalData, sideEffects] = await Promise.allSettled([
  Promise.all([fetchConfig(), authenticate()]),   // critical
  Promise.all([loadAnalytics(), prefetchImages()]) // optional
]);

if (criticalData.status === 'fulfilled') {
  const [config, user] = criticalData.value;
  render(config, user);
}
```

