**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Finite state machines prevent invalid concurrent transitions by making state changes explicit and guarded. Each state has allowed transitions; concurrent attempts to transition from the same state are rejected.

Async state machine:
```javascript
class AsyncStateMachine {
  #state;
  #transitions;
  #transitioning = false;

  constructor(initial, transitions) {
    this.#state = initial;
    this.#transitions = transitions;
  }

  get state() { return this.#state; }

  async transition(event) {
    if (this.#transitioning) {
      throw new Error(`Cannot transition — already transitioning from ${this.#state}`);
    }

    const key = `${this.#state}:${event}`;
    const transition = this.#transitions[key];
    if (!transition) {
      throw new Error(`Invalid transition: ${event} from ${this.#state}`);
    }

    this.#transitioning = true;
    const prevState = this.#state;
    this.#state = transition.target; // update state immediately
    try {
      await transition.action?.(); // async side effects
    } catch (err) {
      this.#state = prevState; // rollback on failure
      throw err;
    } finally {
      this.#transitioning = false;
    }

    return this.#state;
  }
}

// Data fetching machine
const machine = new AsyncStateMachine('idle', {
  'idle:FETCH':     { target: 'loading', action: startFetch },
  'loading:SUCCESS':{ target: 'success', action: updateUI },
  'loading:ERROR':  { target: 'error',   action: showError },
  'error:RETRY':    { target: 'loading', action: startFetch },
  'success:FETCH':  { target: 'loading', action: startFetch },
});

await machine.transition('FETCH');
// Concurrent FETCH while loading:
try {
  await machine.transition('FETCH'); // throws — already transitioning
} catch (e) {
  console.log('Blocked:', e.message);
}
```

