**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

WebSocket messages arrive in order, but if handlers are async, processing order may not match arrival order — later messages get processed before earlier ones if earlier async processing is slow.

Bug — async handlers cause out-of-order processing:
```javascript
ws.onmessage = async (event) => {
  const data = JSON.parse(event.data);
  const processed = await processMessage(data); // slow async processing
  applyToState(processed); // may apply out of order!
};
// Message 1 arrives: starts processMessage(1) — takes 500ms
// Message 2 arrives: starts processMessage(2) — takes 100ms
// Message 2 finishes first: state updated with msg2
// Message 1 finishes: state updated with msg1 — OVERWRITES newer state!
```

Fix — queue messages, process sequentially:
```javascript
class OrderedMessageProcessor {
  #queue = Promise.resolve();

  process(handler) {
    // Chain onto existing queue — each message waits for previous
    this.#queue = this.#queue.then(handler).catch(console.error);
    return this.#queue;
  }
}

const processor = new OrderedMessageProcessor();

ws.onmessage = (event) => {
  const data = JSON.parse(event.data);
  processor.process(async () => {
    const processed = await processMessage(data);
    applyToState(processed);
  });
};
// Now: msg1 starts, msg2 queued, msg2 runs only after msg1 completes
```

Fix with sequence numbers (client-side reordering):
```javascript
const pending = new Map();
let nextExpected = 0;

ws.onmessage = async (event) => {
  const { seq, data } = JSON.parse(event.data);
  const processed = await processMessage(data);
  pending.set(seq, processed);

  // Drain in order
  while (pending.has(nextExpected)) {
    applyToState(pending.get(nextExpected));
    pending.delete(nextExpected);
    nextExpected++;
  }
};
```

