**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Promise.any() resolves with the first fulfilled promise, ignoring rejections. Only rejects (with AggregateError) if ALL input promises reject. Ideal for fallback patterns where any one source succeeding is sufficient.

Fallback across multiple CDNs:
```javascript
const script = await Promise.any([
  fetch('https://cdn1.example.com/lib.js').then(r => r.text()),
  fetch('https://cdn2.example.com/lib.js').then(r => r.text()),
  fetch('https://cdn3.example.com/lib.js').then(r => r.text()),
]);
// Uses whichever CDN responds first successfully
```

First healthy replica:
```javascript
async function queryFirstHealthy(replicas, query) {
  try {
    return await Promise.any(
      replicas.map(replica => replica.query(query))
    );
  } catch (err) {
    // err is AggregateError — contains all rejection reasons
    console.error('All replicas failed:', err.errors);
    throw new Error('No healthy replica available');
  }
}
```

Geolocation with multiple providers:
```javascript
const position = await Promise.any([
  getFromGPS(),
  getFromWifi(),
  getFromCellTower(),
]).catch(e => {
  // AggregateError.errors = [gpsError, wifiError, cellError]
  throw new Error('Could not determine location');
});
```

Key differences:
- Promise.any() — first FULFILLED (ignores rejections)
- Promise.race() — first SETTLED (fulfilled OR rejected)
- Promise.all() — all must fulfill, one reject stops all
- Promise.allSettled() — wait for all, never rejects

Handling AggregateError:
```javascript
try {
  await Promise.any([p1, p2, p3]);
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof AggregateError) {
    err.errors.forEach((e, i) => console.log(`Promise ${i} rejected:`, e));
  }
}
