FinalizationRegistry runs a callback after the garbage collector reclaims a registered object. Used for cleanup of external resources (file handles, native memory) when the JS wrapper is collected.

Basic usage:
```javascript
const registry = new FinalizationRegistry((heldValue) => {
  console.log('Object collected — cleanup:', heldValue);
  releaseNativeResource(heldValue);
});

function createResource(id) {
  const resource = new Resource(id);
  // Register: when resource is GC'd, call cleanup with id as held value
  registry.register(resource, id);
  return resource;
}
```

With unregister token:
```javascript
const registry = new FinalizationRegistry((handle) => {
  nativeLib.closeHandle(handle);
});

class NativeWrapper {
  #token = {}; // unique object used as unregister token
  #handle;

  constructor() {
    this.#handle = nativeLib.openHandle();
    registry.register(this, this.#handle, this.#token);
  }

  dispose() {
    nativeLib.closeHandle(this.#handle);
    registry.unregister(this.#token); // cancel GC callback — already cleaned up
  }
}
```

With WeakRef pair:
```javascript
const weakRefs = new Map();
const registry = new FinalizationRegistry((key) => {
  weakRefs.delete(key); // remove stale WeakRef from map
});

function track(key, obj) {
  const ref = new WeakRef(obj);
  weakRefs.set(key, ref);
  registry.register(obj, key); // cleanup map entry on GC
}
```

Critical warnings:
- Callback timing is completely non-deterministic
- Callbacks may never fire in short-lived programs
- Do NOT use for critical logic — only for optional resource optimization
- Callbacks run in implementation-defined order
- In same turn of event loop, all strong references to object must be gone before GC considers it

