**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

If a for-await-of loop breaks early without the generator's return() being called, the generator may leak resources — open connections, event listeners, timers.

Bug — resource leak on break:
```javascript
async function* openConnections() {
  const conn = await db.connect(); // open connection
  try {
    while (true) {
      yield await conn.readNext();
    }
  } finally {
    await conn.close(); // closes ONLY if generator is properly returned
  }
}

async function main() {
  for await (const item of openConnections()) {
    if (item.done) break; // break triggers return() on generator — finally runs
    process(item);
  }
  // Connection properly closed — break DOES trigger generator.return()
}
```

Bug — leaks when using manual iteration without return():
```javascript
async function* eventSource() {
  const ws = new WebSocket('wss://api.example.com');
  try {
    for await (const msg of wsMessages(ws)) {
      yield msg;
    }
  } finally {
    ws.close(); // cleanup
  }
}

const gen = eventSource();
const { value: first } = await gen.next();
// If we just stop here without calling gen.return() — WebSocket stays open!
// Must explicitly close:
await gen.return(); // triggers finally — WebSocket closed
```

Safe wrapper that ensures cleanup:
```javascript
async function* withCleanup(gen) {
  try {
    yield* gen;
  } finally {
    await gen.return(); // ensure inner generator cleaned up
  }
}

// Or use try/finally with for-await:
async function consume(gen) {
  try {
    for await (const item of gen) {
      if (shouldStop(item)) break;
      process(item);
    }
  } finally {
    await gen.return(); // explicit cleanup even on break/error
  }
}
```

