Async IIFEs (immediately invoked function expressions) at module/script top level are a common pattern — but unhandled rejections inside them silently fail unless errors are caught.

Bad — uncaught rejection:
```javascript
// Module top level — runs immediately
(async () => {
  const data = await fetchCriticalData(); // if this throws — silent failure
  initializeApp(data);
})();
// Rejection is unhandled — app never initializes, no error visible
```

Bad — catch that silently swallows:
```javascript
(async () => {
  const data = await fetchCriticalData();
  initializeApp(data);
})().catch(() => {}); // swallows all errors — impossible to debug
```

Good — explicit error handling:
```javascript
(async () => {
  const data = await fetchCriticalData();
  initializeApp(data);
})().catch((err) => {
  console.error('Fatal initialization error:', err);
  showErrorScreen('Failed to load application');
  // Report to error tracking
  errorTracker.capture(err);
});
```

Better — top-level await (in ES modules):
```javascript
// In .mjs file or <script type="module"> — no IIFE needed
try {
  const data = await fetchCriticalData();
  initializeApp(data);
} catch (err) {
  showErrorScreen('Failed to load');
  throw err; // re-throw so module load fails explicitly
}
```

Pattern — async main with structured error handling:
```javascript
async function main() {
  const [config, session] = await Promise.all([loadConfig(), loadSession()]);
  await initializeApp(config, session);
}

main().catch((err) => {
  document.body.innerHTML = `<h1>Fatal Error</h1><pre>${err.message}</pre>`;
  console.error(err);
});
```

