Mixing callback APIs with promise chains creates unhandled rejection chains, double-callback bugs, and error swallowing. Always promisify callback APIs at the boundary.

Bad — callback inside Promise executor:
```javascript
function badPattern(url) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    fetchWithCallback(url, (err, data) => {
      if (err) reject(err);
      else {
        // If processData throws — exception is SWALLOWED inside callback
        // reject() is never called — promise hangs forever
        const processed = processData(data);
        resolve(processed);
      }
    });
  });
}
```

Bad — mixing .then() and async/await:
```javascript
async function mixed(url) {
  const data = await fetch(url)
    .then(r => r.json())
    .catch(err => {
      console.error(err);
      // returns undefined — next line gets undefined!
    });
  return data.items; // TypeError: Cannot read 'items' of undefined
}
```

Good — consistent promisification:
```javascript
// Wrap callback API once at the boundary
