Promise.race() resolves or rejects with the first promise to settle — fulfilled OR rejected. Distinct from Promise.any() which waits for first fulfilled. Key for implementing timeouts.

Request timeout:
```javascript
function fetchWithTimeout(url, ms) {
  const timeout = new Promise((_, reject) =>
    setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(`Timeout after ${ms}ms`)), ms)
  );
  return Promise.race([fetch(url), timeout]);
}

try {
  const res = await fetchWithTimeout('/api/data', 5000);
  const data = await res.json();
} catch (err) {
  if (err.message.startsWith('Timeout')) {
    showTimeoutError();
  }
}
```

Better timeout with AbortSignal.timeout (no manual reject):
```javascript
const res = await fetch('/api/data', {
  signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000) // cleaner — auto-aborts fetch
});
```

Race between cache and network:
```javascript
async function cacheRace(request) {
  const networkFetch = fetch(request).then(r => r.clone());
  const cacheHit = caches.match(request);

  const winner = await Promise.race([cacheHit, networkFetch]);
  if (winner) return winner; // cache wins if populated
  return networkFetch;       // fallback to network
}
```

Key behaviors:
- Empty iterable: returned promise stays pending forever
- Non-promise value in iterable: settles immediately to that value
- Unlike Promise.any(): a fast rejection wins (race doesn't ignore rejections)

Comparison:
```javascript
const fast = Promise.reject('fast error');
const slow = new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r('slow value'), 1000));

await Promise.race([fast, slow]);  // rejects with 'fast error'
await Promise.any([fast, slow]);   // resolves with 'slow value' (ignores rejection)
```

