Node.js module caching means module-level variables are singletons shared across all requests in the same process. Concurrent requests sharing mutable module-level state cause race conditions.

Bug — shared request state:
```javascript
// api-handler.js
let currentUserId = null; // MODULE-LEVEL — shared across ALL requests!
let requestData = {};

export async function handleRequest(req) {
  currentUserId = req.userId; // Request A sets this
  await validateUser(currentUserId); // Request B overwrites currentUserId here!
  const data = await fetchUserData(currentUserId); // wrong userId!
  return data;
}
```

Fix — keep state in function scope:
```javascript
export async function handleRequest(req) {
  const userId = req.userId; // local variable — not shared
  await validateUser(userId);
  const data = await fetchUserData(userId);
  return data;
}
```

Fix — pass context explicitly:
```javascript
async function processRequest(ctx) {
  const { userId, sessionId, data } = ctx; // all in ctx, not globals
  const user = await fetchUser(userId);
  const result = await processData(data, user);
  return { result, userId };
}
```

When module-level is OK — immutable config/constants:
```javascript
// SAFE — read-only constants shared across requests
const DB_POOL = createPool({ max: 10 }); // connection pool — safe to share
const CONFIG = Object.freeze({ apiUrl: '...' }); // frozen — immutable
const REGEX = /^[a-z]+$/; // compiled once — read-only

// UNSAFE — mutable state
let requestCount = 0; // race condition under concurrent requests
const cache = {}; // concurrent reads/writes — may corrupt
```

Safe shared mutable state — use proper primitives:
```javascript
const requestCount = { value: 0 };
const increment = () => requestCount.value++; // still not atomic — use atomic-sleep or mutex
```

