React batches state updates in event handlers, but async setState calls after await points may not be batched — causing extra renders and stale state captures.

Bug — stale state in closure:
```javascript
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  async function handleClick() {
    // Both read count=0 at time of click
    setCount(count + 1); // schedules set to 1
    await delay(100);
    setCount(count + 1); // count is still 0 in this closure — sets to 1 again!
  }
  // Net result: count = 1 instead of 2
}
```

Fix — functional update (reads current state):
```javascript
async function handleClick() {
  setCount(c => c + 1); // functional — always increments current value
  await delay(100);
  setCount(c => c + 1); // still functional — works correctly
  // Net result: count = 2 — correct
}
```

Bug — multiple non-batched state updates after await:
```javascript
async function loadData() {
  setLoading(true);
  const data = await fetchData();
  // After await — React 17 does NOT batch these:
  setData(data);        // render 1
  setLoading(false);    // render 2 — extra render!
  setError(null);       // render 3 — extra render!
}
```

Fix — React 18 automatic batching (or useReducer):
```javascript
// React 18: automatic batching after await — only one render
async function loadData() {
  setLoading(true);
  const data = await fetchData();
  setData(data);     // }
  setLoading(false); // } batched in React 18 — single render
  setError(null);    // }
}

// React 17: use flushSync for explicit control or useReducer
import { flushSync } from 'react-dom';
flushSync(() => { setData(data); setLoading(false); }); // one render
```

