**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Singleton pattern for async resources — ensure exactly one instance is created even under concurrent initialization attempts. Classic double-check avoids race.

Naive singleton (race condition):
```javascript
let instance = null;

async function getInstance() {
  if (!instance) { // both callers see null simultaneously
    instance = await createExpensiveResource(); // both create!
  }
  return instance; // second creation overwrites first
}

await Promise.all([getInstance(), getInstance()]); // 2 resources created!
```

Fix — promise-based singleton (stores promise, not value):
```javascript
let instancePromise = null;

function getInstance() {
  if (!instancePromise) {
    instancePromise = createExpensiveResource().catch(err => {
      instancePromise = null; // reset on failure — allow retry
      throw err;
    });
  }
  return instancePromise; // both callers share same promise
}

// Even with concurrent calls — only one createExpensiveResource() runs
const [a, b] = await Promise.all([getInstance(), getInstance()]);
console.log(a === b); // true — same instance
```

Class-based async singleton:
```javascript
class DatabaseClient {
  static #instance = null;

  static getInstance() {
    if (!DatabaseClient.#instance) {
      DatabaseClient.#instance = DatabaseClient.#create().catch(err => {
        DatabaseClient.#instance = null; // allow retry on error
        throw err;
      });
    }
    return DatabaseClient.#instance;
  }

  static async #create() {
    const client = new DatabaseClient();
    await client.#connect();
    return client;
  }

  async #connect() {
    this.pool = await createConnectionPool({ host: 'localhost', size: 5 });
  }

  async query(sql) {
    return this.pool.execute(sql);
  }
}

// Concurrent access — all share one connection pool
const [db1, db2] = await Promise.all([
  DatabaseClient.getInstance(),
  DatabaseClient.getInstance(),
]);
```

