**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Optimistic updates apply changes to the UI immediately without waiting for the server, then roll back if the server request fails. Delivers snappy UX with eventual consistency.

Basic optimistic update:
```javascript
async function likePost(postId) {
  // 1. Apply optimistically — user sees immediate feedback
  const previous = state.posts[postId].likes;
  updateUI({ likes: previous + 1, liked: true });

  try {
    // 2. Sync with server in background
    const result = await api.post(`/posts/${postId}/like`);
    // 3. Update with canonical server value (may differ from optimistic)
    updateUI({ likes: result.likes, liked: result.liked });
  } catch (err) {
    // 4. Rollback on failure
    updateUI({ likes: previous, liked: false });
    showToast('Failed to like — please try again');
  }
}
```

With undo support:
```javascript
const pendingOperations = new Map();

async function deleteItem(id) {
  const item = state.items[id];
  const opId = crypto.randomUUID();

  // Optimistic remove
  removeFromUI(id);
  showUndoToast(`Deleted "${item.name}"`, () => cancelDelete(opId, item));

  pendingOperations.set(opId, { type: 'delete', id, item });

  try {
    await api.delete(`/items/${id}`);
    pendingOperations.delete(opId);
  } catch (err) {
    // Auto-rollback if request failed
    addToUI(id, item);
    pendingOperations.delete(opId);
    showToast('Delete failed — item restored');
  }
}

function cancelDelete(opId, item) {
  const op = pendingOperations.get(opId);
  if (!op) return; // already committed or failed
  // Cancel in-flight request
  api.cancelRequest(opId);
  addToUI(item.id, item); // restore
  pendingOperations.delete(opId);
}
```

