**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

JavaScript Arrays and Objects are not thread-safe across async boundaries — concurrent async modifications to the same structure can corrupt data.

Bug — concurrent array push:
```javascript
const results = [];

async function processAndCollect(item) {
  const result = await process(item);
  results.push(result); // concurrent pushes — safe in JS single thread!
  // Actually OK in JS — event loop is single-threaded
  // BUT: index-based operations are NOT safe across awaits:
  const len = results.length;
  await delay(1); // yield
  results[len] = result; // WRONG — another coroutine may have pushed between read and write
}
```

Bug — read-modify-write pattern on object:
```javascript
const counters = {};

async function incrementCounter(key) {
  const current = counters[key] ?? 0; // read
  await doSomething(); // yield — another increment can run here!
  counters[key] = current + 1; // write — lost update if concurrent!
}

await Promise.all([incrementCounter('a'), incrementCounter('a')]);
console.log(counters.a); // 1, expected 2
```

Fix — complete modification before any await:
```javascript
const counters = {};

async function safeIncrement(key) {
  counters[key] = (counters[key] ?? 0) + 1; // synchronous — no await between read and write
  await doSomething(); // await AFTER modification is complete
}
```

Fix — collect via Promise.all, then write once:
```javascript
async function processAll(items) {
  const results = await Promise.all(items.map(process));
  // results is complete array — no concurrent writes
  return results;
}
```

Rule: In JS, modify shared state synchronously (no await between read and write) to avoid lost-update races within the same event loop.

