**Scenario (E-commerce):** Implementing this pattern to handle products efficiently.

Returning a promise from an async function without await means the async wrapper's finally/catch doesn't cover the returned promise — resource leaks and missed errors.

Bug — missing await loses error context:
```javascript
async function getUser(id) {
  try {
    return fetchUser(id); // NOT awaited — returns promise
    // If fetchUser rejects, this try/catch DOES NOT catch it!
  } catch (err) {
    logError(err); // never runs
    throw err;
  }
}

// Caller still gets a rejected promise, but error is NOT logged
await getUser(999); // rejects, but logger missed it
```

Bug — resource leak without await:
```javascript
async function processWithResource() {
  const resource = await acquireResource();
  try {
    return doWork(resource); // NOT awaited — returns promise
    // If doWork rejects, finally runs BEFORE the work is done!
  } finally {
    await resource.release(); // releases BEFORE doWork settles!
  }
}
```

Fix — always await when in try/finally or try/catch:
```javascript
async function getUser(id) {
  try {
    return await fetchUser(id); // await — error caught here
  } catch (err) {
    logError(err); // now runs on rejection
    throw err;
  }
}

async function processWithResource() {
  const resource = await acquireResource();
  try {
    return await doWork(resource); // await — finally runs after work settles
  } finally {
    await resource.release(); // runs after doWork completes or rejects
  }
}
```

When return-without-await is OK:
```javascript
// OK — no try/catch/finally wrapping the return
async function simpleWrapper(id) {
  const validated = validateId(id);
  return fetchUser(validated); // fine — no error handling needed here
}
```

Rule: inside try/catch or try/finally blocks, always await before returning.

