JavaScript object property iteration order is implementation-defined for some cases. Async code that depends on property insertion order may behave differently across engines or after refactoring.

Fragile — assumes insertion order for async coordination:
```javascript
const asyncTasks = {
  fetchUser: fetchUser(),
  fetchOrders: fetchOrders(), // assumed to run in this order
  fetchSettings: fetchSettings(),
};

// Object.entries order is guaranteed in modern JS for string keys
// (insertion order) — but relying on it for async sequencing is fragile
for (const [name, promise] of Object.entries(asyncTasks)) {
  const result = await promise; // sequential — relies on order
  processResult(name, result);
}
```

Fix — explicit array for ordered async operations:
```javascript
const tasks = [
  { name: 'fetchUser', promise: fetchUser() },
  { name: 'fetchOrders', promise: fetchOrders() },
  { name: 'fetchSettings', promise: fetchSettings() },
];

// Order is explicit and guaranteed
const results = await Promise.all(tasks.map(t => t.promise));
const resultMap = Object.fromEntries(tasks.map((t, i) => [t.name, results[i]]));
```

Anti-pattern — object spread loses async result association:
```javascript
// BAD: spread order may not match execution order
const results = await Promise.all(Object.values(asyncTasks));
const keys = Object.keys(asyncTasks);
// keys[i] may not correspond to results[i] if object mutated async
```

Fix — always use Map or array of tuples for ordered key-value async:
```javascript
const taskPairs = [
  ['user', () => fetchUser(id)],
  ['orders', () => fetchOrders(id)],
];

const results = await Promise.all(
  taskPairs.map(([key, fn]) => fn().then(v => [key, v]))
);
const data = new Map(results);
```

